\h 2 Maccabees
\toc1 The Second Document about the Maccabees
\toc2 2 Maccabees
\toc3 2Ma
\mt2 The second document about
\mt1 the Maccabees
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk The Second Document about the Maccabees\bk* gives another account of the struggle to preserve Jewish worship and the temple in Yerushalem (Jerusalem). Its main account overlaps the earlier part of \bk The First Document about the Maccabees\bk*, concentrating on events leading up to and during the leadership of Yehudah (Judas) Makabi. The writer explains that this is a shortened version of a history written in five books by Yason of Kurainai (Cyrene).
\ip The book opens with letters encouraging the celebration of the temple's rededication. It then describes conflict over the high priesthood, the plundering of the temple, and attempts to force the people to abandon God's instructions. Particular attention is given to Eleazar and to seven brothers and their mother, who choose death rather than disobedience and express their hope that God will raise them to life again.
\ip The later chapters follow Yehudah's victories and the recovery of the temple. Throughout the account, prayer, God's discipline and mercy, and help from heaven are closely connected with what happens on the battlefield. The writer wants readers to honour the temple, remember those who remained faithful under suffering, and recognise God as the one who rescues his people.
\ip \bk The Second Document about the Maccabees\bk* is recognised as Deuterocanonical Scripture by the Romaios Catholic, Greek Orthodox, and Russian Orthodox Churches.
\ip Makabi (from which the term Maccabee is derived) is an acronym formed from the first letters of Exodus 15:11: “Mi kamokha ba'elim YHWH” — “Who is like you among the gods, Yahweh?”
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Letters about celebrating the temple's rededication \ior 1:1–2:18\ior*
\io1 The writer explains the purpose of this history \ior 2:19-32\ior*
\io1 The temple's protection, corrupt leaders, and persecution \ior 3:1–6:17\ior*
\io1 Eleazar, the seven brothers, and their mother \ior 6:18–7:42\ior*
\io1 Yehudah's victories and the rededication of the temple \ior 8:1–10:8\ior*
\io1 Further campaigns, agreements, and the defeat of Nikanor \ior 10:9–15:36\ior*
\io1 The writer's closing words \ior 15:37-39\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 An invitation to celebrate the temple's rededication
\p
\v 1 The kindred, the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* who are in¦80579 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* and¦80582 those who are in¦80584 the¦80585 country¦80586 of Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add*, send greetings and good peace¦80589 to the kindred, the Yehudim who are throughout Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add*.
\v 2 May God¦80595 do¦80592 good to you¦80593, and¦80596 remember his covenant¦80599 with¦80602 Avraham, Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add*, and Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add*, his faithful¦80612 servants,
\v 3 and¦80613 give¦80614 you¦80615 all¦80617 a heart to worship him and do¦80623 his will¦80626 with a strong heart¦80627 and¦80629 a willing soul¦80630.
\v 4 May God open your heart¦80635 to his law¦80639 and¦80641 his statutes, and¦80645 make¦80647 peace¦80646,
\v 5 and¦80648 listen to your requests, and¦80653 be reconciled with you¦80655, and¦80656 not¦80657 forsake you¦80658 in¦80660 an evil¦80662 time¦80661.
\v 6 Now¦80664 we're¦80666 praying¦80667 for you¦80669 here¦80665.
\p
\v 7 In Demetrios' reign, in the¦80692 one hundred and sixty-ninth year¦80693, we¦80676 the¦80677 Yehudim have already written to you¦80680 in¦80681 the¦80682 suffering and¦80684 in¦80685 the¦80686 distress that's¦80688 come on us in¦80691 these years, from¦80695 the time that¦80696 Yason¦80698 and¦80699 his company revolted from¦80703 the¦80704 holy¦80705 land¦80706 and¦80707 the¦80708 kingdom¦80709,
\v 8 and¦80710 set the¦80712 gate¦80713 on fire, and¦80714 shed innocent¦80717 blood¦80716. We prayed to the¦80720 master, and were heard. We offered sacrifices¦80726 and meal offerings. We lit the¦80731 lamps. We set out¦80715 the¦80735 show bread¦80736.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Grk. \fqa loaves\f*
\v 9 Now see that you keep the days of the feast of tabernacles in the month Kislev in the one hundred and eighty-eighth year.
\s1 A second letter to the Yehudim in Mitsrayim
\b
\p
\v 10 The people of Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* and those who are in Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add*, with the senate and Yehudah, to Aristobulos, King Ptolemaios's \add !(Ptolemy)\add* teacher, who's also of the stock of the anointed priests, and to the Yehudim who are in Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add*, we send greetings and health.
\p
\v 11 Having been saved¦80793 by¦80790 God¦80792 out¦80787 of great¦80788 perils, as men arrayed against a king¦80800, we thank¦80795 him greatly¦80794.
\v 12 Because he¦80802 threw out into Paras \add !(Persia)\add* those who¦80805 fought against us in¦80807 the¦80808 holy¦80809 city¦80810.
\v 13 Because¦80812 when¦80818 the¦80813 prince had come there, with an army that seemed irresistible, they were¦80815 cut to pieces in¦80827 Nanaia's temple¦80831 by the treachery of Nanaia's priests¦80838.
\v 14 Because¦80855 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, on the pretence that he'd¦80842 marry her, came¦80843 into the¦80845 place¦80846, he and his friends¦80854 who¦80851 were with¦80852 him, that they might take¦80857 a large part of the¦80858 treasures as a dowry.
\v 15 And¦80863 when the¦80866 priests¦80867 of Nanaia's temple had set the¦80868 treasures out, and he'd come there with¦80872 a small¦80873 company within the¦80875 wall of¦80877 the¦80877 sacred precinct, they locked the¦80880 temple¦80881 when Antiokos had come in.
\v 16 Opening¦80885 the¦80886 secret door¦80890 of¦80887 the¦80887 panelled ceiling, they threw¦80891 stones and struck down the¦80894 prince. They cut him and¦80896 his company in pieces, and¦80899 cut off their heads¦80901, and threw them to the¦80900 people who¦80903 were outside¦80904.
\v 17 Blessed be our god¦80911 in all¦80907 things, who¦80910 handed over those who'd¦80914 committed impiety.
\s1 The recovery of the altar's sacred fire
\p
\v 18 Since we're now about to celebrate the purification¦80926 of¦80927 the¦80927 temple¦80928 in the month Kislev, on the twenty-fifth day, we thought it necessary¦80929 to notify you, so¦80934 that¦80937 you may also¦80939 keep a feast of¦80940 tabernacles, and remember the¦80940 fire¦80941 which was given when¦80942 Nehemyah \add !(Nehemiah)\add* offered sacrifices¦80952, after he'd built both the temple¦80947 and¦80948 the¦80949 altar¦80950.
\p
\v 19 Because¦80954 indeed when¦80955 our fathers¦80961 were about to¦80959 be led into the¦80960 land of Paras, the¦80963 godly priests¦80966 of that time took¦80967 some of¦80969 the¦80969 fire¦80970 of¦80971 the¦80971 altar¦80972, and hid it secretly in¦80975 the hollow of a well¦80977 that was without water, where they made sure that the¦80988 place¦80989 was unknown to anyone.
\v 20 Now after many years, when it pleased God, Nehemyah, having received a charge from the king of Paras, sent in quest of the fire the descendants of the priests who hid it. When they declared to us that they'd found no fire, but thick liquid,
\v 21 he commanded them to draw some of it out and bring it to him. When the sacrifices had been offered, Nehemyah commanded the priests to sprinkle with that liquid both the wood and the things laid on it.
\v 22 When¦81055 that was¦81050 done and¦81052 some time¦81053 had passed¦81054, and the sun¦81056 shone out, which¦81048 before¦81058 was¦81060 hidden with clouds, a great¦81063 blaze was kindled¦81061, so¦81064 that all¦81066 men marvelled¦81065.
\v 23 The¦81070 priests¦81071 made a prayer¦81067 while the¦81073 sacrifice¦81074 was being consumed—both the priests¦81077 and¦81078 all¦81079 the¦81082 others. Yonatan \add !(Jonathan)\add* led and¦81083 the rest responded, as Nehemyah did.
\p
\v 24 The¦81090 prayer¦81091 was like this: “O master, master God¦81099, the¦81100 Creator¦81102 of all¦81101 things, who¦81103 are awesome, strong¦81106, righteous, and merciful¦81110, who¦81111 alone¦81112 are King¦81113 and¦81114 gracious,
\v 25 who¦81116 alone¦81117 supply every need, who¦81119 alone¦81120 are righteous, almighty¦81123, and¦81124 eternal¦81125, you who save Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* out¦81130 of all¦81131 evil¦81132, who¦81133 chose the¦81135 ancestors and¦81138 sanctified¦81139 them,
\v 26 accept the¦81142 sacrifice¦81143 for¦81144 all¦81145 your people¦81147 Yisrael, and¦81150 preserve your own portion, and¦81155 consecrate it.
\v 27 Gather together our scattered people, set at liberty those who are in¦81164 bondage among the heathen, look¦81171 on¦81171 those who are despised and abhorred, and let the¦81174 heathen know that¦81176 you're¦81178 our god¦81180.
\v 28 Punish those who¦81183 oppress us and¦81185 in¦81187 arrogance shamefully entreat us.
\v 29 Plant your people¦81191 in your holy¦81197 place¦81195, even as¦81199 Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* said¦81200.”
\p
\v 30 Then¦81203 the¦81202 priests¦81204 sang the¦81206 hymns.
\v 31 As¦81208 soon as¦81208 the¦81212 sacrifice¦81213 was consumed, then¦81214 Nehemyah \add !(Nehemiah)\add* commanded that the¦81215 rest of the¦81218 liquid be poured on large¦81222 stones¦81221.
\v 32 When¦81225 this¦81226 was¦81227 done, a flame¦81228 was kindled¦81229; but when the light¦81236 from¦81232 the¦81233 altar¦81234 shone back, it went out.
\v 33 When¦81239 the¦81242 matter became known, and¦81244 it was told the¦81246 king¦81247 of the¦81248 Persians that¦81250, in the¦81252 place¦81253 where the¦81255 priests¦81260 who¦81261 were led away¦81264 had hid the fire¦81256, the¦81258 liquid appeared¦81263 which¦81265 Nehemyah and¦81266 those who were with him purified the¦81273 sacrifice¦81274,
\v 34 then the¦81277 king¦81278 enclosed the place and made¦81280 it sacred after he'd investigated the¦81282 matter.
\v 35 When¦81284 the¦81287 king¦81288 would show favour to any, he'd exchange many presents and¦81292 give them some of this liquid.
\v 36 Nehemyah and¦81295 those who were with him called this thing “Nephthar”, which¦81302 is by interpretation, “Cleansing¦81304”; but most men call¦81305 it Nephthai.
\c 2
\s1 Yirmiyah hides the sacred tent and chest
\p
\v 1 It's also¦81312 found in¦81313 the¦81314 records that¦81319 the¦81317 prophet¦81318 Yirmiyah \add !(Jeremiah)\add* commanded those who were carried away to¦81320 take¦81323 some of¦81321 the¦81321 fire¦81322, as has been mentioned,
\v 2 and¦81328 how¦81329 that¦81331 the¦81333 prophet¦81334 charged those who were carried away, having given¦81335 them the¦81337 law¦81338, that¦81339 they shouldn't¦81340 forget the¦81342 statutes of¦81344 the¦81344 master or be led astray in¦81347 their minds when they saw¦81352 images of gold¦81354 and¦81355 silver¦81356, and¦81357 their adornment.
\v 3 With other¦81363 such¦81364 words exhorted he them, that¦81364 the¦81369 law¦81370 shouldn't¦81367 depart from¦81371 their hearts¦81373.
\p
\v 4 It was¦81375 in¦81377 the¦81378 writing¦81379 that the¦81387 prophet¦81388, being warned by God, commanded that the tabernacle and¦81383 the¦81384 ark¦81385 should follow with him,\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Grk. \fqa and when. \ft The Greek text here is probably corrupt.\f* when he¦81391 went¦81395 out¦81395 to the¦81397 mountain¦81398 where Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* had gone up and saw God's¦81406 inheritance¦81407.
\v 5 Yirmiyah came¦81409 and found¦81412 a cave, he brought the¦81416 tabernacle, the¦81419 ark¦81420, and¦81421 the¦81422 altar¦81423 of¦81424 incense¦81425 into it; then¦81428 he sealed the¦81429 entrance¦81430.
\v 6 Some of those who followed with him came¦81433 there that¦81435 they might mark the¦81439 way, and¦81441 couldn't¦81443 find¦81444 it.
\v 7 But¦81446 when¦81454 Yirmiyah learnt about¦81465 that¦81453, he¦81451 rebuked them, saying¦81452, “The¦81456 place¦81457 will be¦81458 unknown¦81455 until¦81459 God¦81463 gathers¦81464 the people¦81466 together again and¦81467 shows mercy.
\v 8 Then¦81471 the¦81472 master will disclose these things, and¦81476 the¦81480 master's glory¦81479 will be seen¦81477 with the¦81483 cloud¦81484, as it was also¦81486 shown to¦81487 Mosheh, also¦81491 as Shelomoh \add !(Solomon)\add* implored that¦81495 the¦81496 place¦81497 might be consecrated greatly¦81499,
\v 9 and¦81501 it was also¦81502 declared that¦81503 he, having wisdom¦81504, offered a sacrifice¦81507 of dedication, and¦81509 of the¦81510 finishing of¦81512 the¦81512 temple¦81513.
\v 10 As¦81514 Mosheh prayed¦81517 to¦81518 the master and¦81520 fire came¦81521 down¦81521 out¦81523 of¦81524 heaven¦81525 and¦81526 consumed the¦81528 sacrifice¦81529, even so Shelomoh also¦81532 prayed¦81534, and¦81535 the¦81537 fire¦81538 came down and consumed the¦81540 burnt¦81541 offerings¦81541.
\v 11 Mosheh\f + \fr 2:11 \ft See Leviticus 10:16 and 9:24.\f* said¦81543, ‘Because¦81545 the¦81551 sin¦81552 offering hadn't been eaten, it was consumed in the same way.’
\v 12 Likewise¦81554 Shelomoh kept the¦81558 eight¦81559 days¦81560.”
\p
\v 13 The same things were reported both in¦81565 the¦81566 public archives and¦81568 in¦81569 Nehemyah's \add !(Nehemiah)\add* records, and¦81578 also¦81578 how¦81579 he, founding a library, gathered together the¦81583 books about¦81584 the¦81585 kings¦81586 and¦81587 prophets¦81588, and¦81589 the¦81590 writings of¦81591 David, and¦81593 letters of kings¦81595 about¦81596 sacred gifts.
\v 14 In the same way Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* also gathered together for us all¦81611 those books that'd been scattered because¦81604 of¦81604 the¦81605 war¦81606, and¦81612 they're¦81613 still with¦81614 us.
\v 15 If¦81618 therefore¦81617 you have need¦81619 of them, send some people to bring them to you¦81623.
\p
\v 16 Seeing then¦81626 that we're about to¦81627 celebrate the¦81628 purification¦81629, we write to you¦81631. You'll therefore¦81633 do¦81634 well¦81632 if you celebrate the¦81636 days¦81637.
\v 17 Now God¦81640, who¦81641 saved¦81642 all¦81644 his people¦81645, and¦81647 restored the¦81649 heritage¦81650 to all¦81651, with the¦81653 kingdom, the¦81656 priesthood¦81657, and¦81658 the¦81659 consecration,
\v 18 even as¦81661 he promised through¦81663 the¦81664 law¦81665—in God¦81670 have we hope¦81666, that¦81671 he'll soon¦81672 have mercy on¦81668 us, and¦81675 gather¦81676 us together out¦81677 of everywhere under¦81679 heaven¦81681 into his holy¦81684 place¦81685; for¦81687 he delivered us out¦81689 of great¦81690 evils¦81691, and¦81692 purified the¦81693 place¦81694.
\b
\pc ———————
\s1 The writer explains the purpose of this history
\b
\p
\v 19 Now the things about Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* and his brothers¦81706, the¦81708 purification¦81713 of¦81709 the¦81709 greatest¦81712 temple¦81710, the dedication of¦81716 the¦81716 altar,
\v 20 and¦81720 further the wars¦81731 against Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* Epifanes \add !(Epiphanes)\add* and¦81726 Eupator his son¦81729,
\v 21 and¦81732 the¦81733 manifestations that came from¦81734 heaven¦81735 to those who¦81738 fought with one another in brave actions for¦81739 the religion of¦81740 the¦81740 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*; so¦81744 that, being but a few, they seized the¦81745 whole¦81746 country¦81747, chased the¦81752 barbarous multitudes¦81754,
\v 22 recovered again the¦81757 temple¦81763 renowned all¦81760 the¦81761 world¦81762 over, freed the¦81766 city¦81767, and¦81769 restored the¦81770 laws¦81773 which were about¦81775 to be overthrown, seeing the master became gracious to them with¦81777 all¦81778 kindness.
\v 23 These things which have been declared by¦81784 Yason of Kurainai \add !(Cyrene)\add* in five¦81790 books, we'll attempt to abridge in one¦81794 book.
\v 24 Because¦81814 having in view the¦81799 confused mass of the¦81801 numbers, and¦81803 the¦81804\f + \fr 2:24 \ft Or, weariness\f* difficulty which awaits those who'd¦81808 enter into the¦81810 narratives of the¦81811 history, because of the¦81815 abundance of the¦81817 matter,
\v 25 we were careful that those who¦81825 choose to read may be attracted, and¦81826 that those who¦81829 wish us well may find it easy to recall, and¦81835 that all¦81834 readers may benefit.
\v 26 Although to us, who've taken on¦81841 ourselves the¦81842 painful labour of the¦81843 abridgement, the¦81846 task isn't easy, but¦81851 a matter of sweat and¦81852 sleeplessness,
\v 27 even as¦81856 it's no¦81865 light thing to him who¦81857 prepares a banquet, and¦81860 seeks¦81861 the¦81862 benefit of others¦81863. Nevertheless, for¦81869 the sake of¦81869 the¦81870 gratitude of the¦81871 many we'll gladly¦81874 endure the¦81875 painful labour,
\v 28 leaving to the historian the exact handling of every particular, and¦81887 again having no strength to fill in the outlines of our abridgement.
\v 29 Because as¦81894 the¦81896 master-builder of a new¦81897 house¦81898 must care for the¦81900 whole¦81901 structure, and again he who¦81910 undertakes to¦81912 decorate and¦81917 paint it must seek out the things fit for its adorning; even so I¦81919 think¦81916 it's also with us.
\v 30 To occupy the¦81920 ground, and¦81923 to¦81926 indulge in long discussions, and¦81928 to be curious in¦81930 particulars, is fitting for the first author of the history;
\v 31 but¦81939 to strive after brevity of expression, and¦81944 to avoid a laboured fullness in the treatment, is to be granted to him who'd bring a writing into a new form.
\v 32 Here¦81955 then¦81956 let's begin the¦81958 narration, only adding this¦81960 much to that which has already been said; for¦81965 it's a foolish thing to make a long prologue to the history, and¦81973 to abridge the history itself.
\b
\c 3
\s1 Heliodoros tries to seize the temple's money
\p
\v 1 When the¦81976 holy¦81977 city¦81979 was inhabited with¦81981 unbroken peace¦81983 and¦81984 the¦81985 laws were kept very well because¦81990 of¦81990 the¦81991 godliness of¦81993 Honyo \add !(Onias)\add* the¦81993 high priest and his hatred of wickedness,
\v 2 it¦82001 happened¦81999 that even the¦82002 kings¦82003 themselves honoured the¦82005 place¦82006 and¦82007 glorified¦82013 the¦82008 temple¦82009 with the¦82011 noblest presents,
\v 3 so¦82014 that even King¦82020 Seleukos \add !(Seleucus)\add* of Asia bore all¦82026 the¦82027 costs belonging to¦82028 the¦82029 services of the¦82031 sacrifices¦82032 out¦82022 of his own¦82024 revenues.
\v 4 But¦82036 a man¦82037 named Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add* of the¦82039 tribe¦82041 of Benyamin, having been made guardian of the¦82043 temple¦82044, disagreed with the¦82047 high priest about¦82049 the¦82050 ruling of the market in the¦82052 city¦82053.
\v 5 When¦82055 he couldn't overcome Honyo, he went¦82061 to¦82062 Apolonios of Tarsos,\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Greek \fqa Thraseas\f* who at that¦82067 time¦82069 was governor of Coele-Syria and¦82072 Foinikai.
\v 6 He¦82096 brought him word¦82101 how that the treasury¦82085 in Yerushalem was full of untold sums of money¦82079, so¦82086 that the¦82087 multitude¦82088 of the¦82089 funds was¦82092 innumerable, and¦82093 that they didn't¦82094 pertain to¦82097 the¦82098 account of the¦82099 sacrifices¦82100, but¦82103 that it was possible that these should fall under¦82105 the king's¦82108 power.
\v 7 When¦82114 Apolonios met the king, he informed him of the money about¦82119 which he'd¦82122 been told. So¦82126 the¦82129 king appointed Heliodoros, who¦82131 was his chancellor, and sent¦82133 him with a command to¦82134 accomplish the¦82136 removal of the¦82137 reported money¦82139.
\v 8 So¦82143 Heliodoros set out on¦82150 his journey at once, ostensibly to visit the cities¦82159 of¦82165 Coele-Syria and Foinikai, but in fact to execute the¦82165 king's¦82166 purpose¦82167.
\p
\v 9 When¦82170 he'd come to Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* and¦82173 had been courteously received by¦82175 the high priest of the city¦82179, he told him about¦82182 the¦82183 information which¦82187 had been given, and declared why he'd come; and he enquired if¦82193 in truth these things were so.
\v 10 The¦82200 high priest explained to¦82205 him that there were¦82205 in the treasury deposits of¦82200 widows¦82206 and orphans,
\v 11 and moreover some money¦82231 belonging to Hurkanos the son of Toviyah \add !(Tobias)\add*, a man¦82217 in¦82218 very¦82216 high place, not as¦82222 that impious Shim'on falsely alleged; and¦82229 that in all¦82230 there were four¦82232 hundred¦82232 talents¦82233 of silver and two hundred of gold¦82234,
\v 12 and¦82238 that it was altogether impossible that wrong should be done to¦82240 those who'd put trust¦82240 in¦82240 the¦82241 holiness¦82244 of¦82242 the¦82242 place¦82243, and¦82245 in the¦82246 majesty and inviolable sanctity of¦82247 the¦82247 temple¦82253, honoured¦82248 over all the¦82250 world¦82252.
\v 13 But¦82261 Heliodoros, because¦82263 of¦82263 the king's command¦82269 given him, said that¦82271 in any case this¦82274 money must be¦82275 confiscated for the king's treasury.
\p
\v 14 So¦82277 having appointed a day¦82278, he entered¦82279 in to direct the¦82280 enquiry about¦82281 these matters; and¦82286 there was¦82285 no¦82287 small¦82288 distress throughout the¦82291 whole¦82290 city¦82292.
\v 15 The¦82294 priests¦82296, prostrating themselves before¦82297 the¦82298 altar¦82299 in¦82300 their priestly clothes, and called towards¦82307 heaven¦82308 on him who¦82309 gave the¦82313 law about¦82310 deposits, that he¦82315 should preserve these treasures safe for those who'd deposited them.
\v 16 Anyone who saw the appearance of the high priest was¦82318 wounded in mind¦82327; for¦82329 his face¦82330 and¦82331 the change of his colour betrayed the distress of his soul¦82339.
\v 17 Because¦82351 a terror and¦82348 a shuddering¦82349 of the body¦82350 had come over the¦82344 man¦82345, by which¦82352 the¦82355 pain¦82361 that was in his heart¦82359 was plainly shown to¦82356 those who¦82357 looked at him.
\v 18 Those who were in the houses rushed out¦82364 in crowds to¦82369 make a universal supplication, because¦82372 the¦82373 place¦82379 was about¦82372 to¦82377 come¦82377 into dishonour.
\v 19 The women¦82386, girded with sackcloth¦82387 under¦82382 their breasts, thronged the streets. The virgins¦82396 who¦82397 were kept indoors ran together, some to¦82400 the¦82401 gates¦82402, others to¦82405 the¦82406 walls¦82407, and¦82409 some looked out through¦82410 the¦82411 windows.
\v 20 All¦82414, stretching out their hands¦82418 towards¦82419 heaven¦82421, made¦82422 their solemn supplication.
\v 21 Then¦82426 it was¦82427 pitiful to¦82427 see the¦82429 multitude¦82430 prostrating themselves all mixed together, and the anxiety of¦82435 the¦82435 high priest in his great¦82436 distress.
\s1 God protects the temple treasury
\p
\v 22 While therefore¦82442 they called on the¦82444 Almighty master to keep the¦82447 things entrusted to them safe and secure\f + \fr 3:22 \ft Grk. \fqa safe with all security.\f* for those who'd¦82449 entrusted them,
\v 23 Heliodoros went on to execute that which had been decreed.
\v 24 But¦82463 when he¦82464 was already¦82471 present¦82472 there with his guards near the¦82469 treasury¦82470, the Sovereign of spirits and¦82477 of all¦82478 authority¦82479 caused a great¦82482 manifestation, so¦82484 that¦82462 all¦82485 who'd¦82486 presumed to¦82488 come with him, stricken with dismay at God's¦82492 power¦82493, fainted in terror.
\v 25 Because they saw¦82499 a horse¦82502 with a frightening¦82504 rider, adorned with beautiful trappings, and¦82508 he rushed fiercely and¦82513 struck at Heliodoros with his forefeet. It seemed like he who sat on the horse had complete¦82526 armour¦82526 of gold.
\v 26 Two¦82530 others¦82528 also¦82529 appeared to him, young men notable in their strength, and¦82539 beautiful in their glory¦82541, and¦82543 splendid in their clothing, who stood by him on either side, and¦82547 scourged him unceasingly¦82554, inflicting on him many sore stripes.
\v 27 When he'd fallen suddenly¦82559 to the¦82563 ground¦82564, and¦82565 great darkness¦82567 had come over him, his guards picked him up and¦82570 put him on a¦82572 stretcher,
\v 28 and carried him—this¦82574 man who'd just now¦82575 entered¦82585 with¦82576 a great retinue and¦82579 all¦82580 his guard into the¦82583 aforesaid treasury¦82586, himself¦82589 now brought to utter helplessness, manifestly made to recognise the¦82592 sovereignty of¦82593 God¦82594.
\v 29 So¦82597, while he, through¦82600 the working of God, speechless and¦82605 bereft of all¦82606 hope¦82608 and¦82609 deliverance, lay prostrate,
\v 30 they blessed the master who acted marvellously for his own place¦82621. The¦82623 temple¦82630, which a little¦82624 before¦82625 was¦82629 full¦82629 of¦82631 terror and alarm, was filled with joy¦82635 and¦82636 gladness¦82637 after the¦82631 Almighty¦82632 master appeared.
\p
\v 31 But quickly¦82639 some of¦82643 Heliodoros's familiar friends implored Honyo \add !(Onias)\add* to¦82649 call¦82649 on¦82649 the Most High to¦82654 grant life¦82654 to him who lay quite at the last¦82659 gasp.
\v 32 The¦82665 high priest, secretly afraid that the¦82669 king¦82670 might think that¦82675 some treachery towards Heliodoros had been perpetrated by¦82677 the¦82678 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, brought a sacrifice¦82682 for¦82683 the¦82684 recovery of¦82685 the¦82685 man¦82686.
\v 33 But¦82689 as the¦82690 high priest was making the¦82692 atoning sacrifice, the¦82694 same young men appeared¦82698 again¦82697 to¦82698 Heliodoros, arrayed in¦82701 the¦82702 same clothes. They stood and¦82706 said¦82708, “Give¦82720 Honyo the¦82710 high priest great thanks, for his sake the¦82712 master has¦82715 granted you¦82719 life¦82722.
\v 34 See that you¦82725, since you've been scourged from heaven, proclaim to all¦82731 men the¦82732 sovereign majesty of¦82734 God¦82735.” When¦82738 they'd spoken these words, they vanished out of sight.
\v 35 So¦82743 Heliodoros, having offered a sacrifice¦82745 to¦82746 the¦82747 master and¦82749 vowed great\f + \fr 3:35 \ft Grk. \fqa greatest.\f* vows to¦82755 him who'd saved his life¦82755, and¦82757 having bidden Honyo farewell, returned with his army to the¦82763 king¦82764.
\v 36 He testified to all¦82767 men the works¦82773 of¦82774 the¦82774 greatest God¦82776, which he'd seen with his eyes.
\p
\v 37 When¦82778 the¦82777 king¦82779 asked Heliodoros what¦82783 sort of man¦82784 was fit to¦82785 be¦82785 sent yet once¦82788 again to Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, he said,
\v 38 “If¦82793 you have¦82795 any enemy or¦82797 conspirator against the state, send¦82800 him there¦82802, and¦82803 you'll receive him back well scourged, if he even escapes with his life; because¦82810 truly¦82815 there's¦82816 some power¦82819 of God¦82818 in that place¦82814.
\v 39 Because he¦82820 who has his dwelling in heaven¦82825 himself has¦82826 his eyes on that¦82831 place¦82833 and¦82834 helps it. Those who¦82835 come to¦82837 hurt it, he strikes and destroys.”
\p
\v 40 This was the history of Heliodoros and¦82846 the¦82847 keeping¦82850 of¦82848 the¦82848 treasury¦82849.
\c 4
\s1 Conflict over the high priesthood
\p
\v 1 The¦82853 previously mentioned Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add*, who'd¦82857 given information about the¦82858 money¦82859 against his country, slandered Honyo \add !(Onias)\add*, saying that it was he who'd¦82872 incited Heliodoros and¦82875 had been the¦82876 real cause of these evils¦82877.
\v 2 He dared to call him a conspirator against the state who was actually the benefactor of the¦82883 city¦82884, the guardian of his fellow countrymen, and¦82890 a zealot¦82891 for the laws.
\v 3 When¦82900 his hatred grew so¦82903 great¦82903 that even murders were perpetrated through¦82907 one¦82908 of¦82911 Shim'on's approved agents,
\v 4 Honyo, seeing the danger of the contention, and¦82923 that Apolonios\f + \fr 4:4 \ft Compare 2 Maccabees 4:21. See also 2 Maccabees 3:5. The Greek as commonly read means \fqa Apollonius, as \ft being \fqa the governor…Phoenicia, did rage, and increase etc.\f* the son of Menestheus, the governor of Coele-Syria and Foinikai, was increasing Shim'on's malice,
\v 5 appealed to the¦82939 king¦82940, not to be an accuser¦82946 of his fellow-citizens, but¦82948 looking to the good of all¦82953 the¦82954\f + \fr 4:5 \ft Grk. \fqa multitude.\f* people, both public and private¦82952;
\v 6 for¦82958 he saw that without¦82959 the king's involvement it was¦82963 impossible for the state to¦82964 obtain¦82964 peace¦82965 any more, and¦82969 that Shim'on wouldn't cease from his madness.
\s1 Yason introduces Greek customs
\p
\v 7 When Seleukos \add !(Seleucus)\add* was deceased, and¦82978 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, who¦82979 was called Epifanes \add !(Epiphanes)\add*, succeeded to the¦82984 kingdom¦82985, Yason¦82991 the¦82992 brother of Honyo supplanted his brother in the¦82995 high priesthood,
\v 8 having promised to¦82997 the¦82998 king¦82999 at an audience three hundred and sixty¦83004 talents of silver, and¦83008 out of another¦83011 fund eighty¦83013 talents.
\v 9 In addition to¦83014 this, he undertook to¦83022 assign one hundred¦83024 and fifty¦83021 more, if¦83025 it might be allowed him through¦83027\f + \fr 4:9 \ft Grk. \fqa through his.\f* the¦83028 king's authority¦83029 to set him up a gymnasium and¦83032 a body of youths to be trained in it¦83034, and¦83036 to register the¦83037 inhabitants of Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* as citizens of Antiokeia.
\v 10 When¦83043 the¦83044 king¦83045 had assented, and¦83046 Yason had taken¦83049 possession of the¦83047 office, he immediately¦83050 shifted those of his own race to¦83051 the Greek way of life.
\v 11 Setting aside the¦83059 royal ordinances of special favour to¦83060 the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, granted by the means of Yohan \add !(John)\add* the¦83067 father¦83068 of¦83070 Eupolemos, who went on¦83084 the¦83070 mission to the¦83072 Romaios to establish friendship and alliance, and seeking to¦83078 overthrow the¦83079 lawful ways of living, he brought in new customs forbidden by the¦83083 law.
\v 12 Because¦83092 he¦83094 eagerly established a gymnasium under the¦83095 citadel itself, and¦83099 caused the¦83100 noblest of the¦83102 young men to¦83107 wear the Greek hat.
\v 13 Thus there was¦83108 an extreme of hellenisation, and¦83109 an advance of a foreign religion, because¦83117 of¦83117 the exceeding profaneness of Yason, who was an ungodly man¦83112 and¦83121 not a high priest;
\v 14 so¦83127 that¦83130 the¦83136 priests¦83137 had no more any zeal for the services of¦83131 the¦83131 altar¦83132; but¦83138 despising the¦83139 sanctuary and¦83143 neglecting the¦83144 sacrifices¦83145, they hastened to¦83148 enjoy that¦83149 which was unlawfully provided in¦83150 the wrestling arena, after¦83154 the¦83155 summons to the¦83156 discus-throwing.
\v 15 They despised the honours¦83163 of their fathers, and¦83159 valued the¦83160 prestige of the¦83167 Hellains \add !(Greeks)\add* best of all.
\v 16 Because¦83174 this reason, severe calamity overtook them. The men whose ways of¦83174 living they earnestly followed, and to¦83186 whom they desired¦83186 to be made like in all things, these became their enemies and punished them.
\v 17 Because¦83194 it isn't a light thing to show irreverence to God's laws¦83198, but¦83201 later events will make¦83206 this clear¦83206.
\p
\v 18 Now when¦83208 certain games that came every fifth year were kept at Tsor \add !(Tyre)\add*, and¦83213 the¦83214 king¦83215 was¦83216 present¦83216,
\v 19 the vile Yason¦83218 sent¦83217 sacred envoys,\f + \fr 4:19 \ft See ver. 9.\f* as being¦83225 Antiochians of Yerushalem, bearing three hundred drachmas¦83228 of¦83232 silver to the¦83232 sacrifice¦83234 of Herakles, which¦83235 even those carrying it didn't¦83240 think was right to¦83242 use for any sacrifice¦83243, because¦83244 it wasn't¦83246 fit, but¦83250 to¦83251 spend it for another purpose.
\v 20 Although the intended purpose of¦83256 the¦83258 sender this¦83261 money was for the¦83262 sacrifice¦83264 of Herakles, yet on account of present\f + \fr 4:20 \ft Some authorities read \fqa the bearers.\f* circumstances it went to the construction of trireme warships.
\p
\v 21 Now when¦83275 Apolonios the¦83279 son of¦83281 Menestheus was sent into Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add* for the¦83282 enthronement\f + \fr 4:21 \ft The exact meaning of the Greek word is uncertain.\f* of Filometor as king¦83287, Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, learning that¦83296 Filometor had shown himself¦83298 hostile towards¦83302 the government, took precautions for the security of his realm. Therefore, going to¦83304 Yafo \add !(Joppa)\add*, he travelled on to¦83305 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 22 Being magnificently received by¦83310 Yason and¦83313 the¦83314 city¦83315, he was brought in with¦83317 torches and¦83319 shouting. Then he led his army down into Foinikai.
\s1 Menelaos buys the high priesthood
\p
\v 23 Now after¦83328 a space of three years, Yason¦83333 sent¦83332 Menelaos, the previously mentioned Shim'on's \add !(Simon)\add* brother¦83339, to carry the¦83341 money¦83342 to the¦83343 king¦83344, and¦83345 to make reports about¦83346 some necessary matters.
\v 24 But¦83352 he being commended to the¦83354 king¦83355, and¦83356 having been glorified by the display of his authority¦83362, secured the high priesthood for himself¦83364, outbidding Yason by three hundred talents¦83371 of silver.
\v 25 After receiving the¦83376 royal mandates, he returned¦83379 bringing nothing worthy¦83384 of the high priesthood, but¦83387 having the passion of a cruel tyrant¦83389 and¦83390 the rage of a savage animal.
\v 26 So Yason¦83398, who'd supplanted his own¦83401 brother¦83402, was supplanted by¦83405 another and driven as a fugitive into the¦83409 country¦83411 of the Amonim,
\v 27 Menelaos¦83415 had possession of the¦83413 office; but¦83414 of the¦83416 money¦83425 that'd¦83420 been promised to the¦83423 king¦83424 nothing was regularly paid, even though Sostratos the governor of the citadel demanded it—
\v 28 for¦83439 his job was¦83440 the gathering of¦83445 the¦83448 revenues—so¦83429 they were both¦83449 called by¦83450 the¦83451 king¦83452 to his presence.
\v 29 Menelaos¦83457 left his own brother¦83465 Lusimakos for his\f + \fr 4:29 \ft Grk. \fqa successor.\f* deputy in the high priesthood; and Sostratos¦83466 left Krates, who¦83469 was over the¦83471 Cyprians.
\s1 The murder of Honyo
\p
\v 30 Now while this¦83473 was the state of things, it happened¦83476 that the people of Tarsos and¦83478 Malos revolted because¦83481 they were to be given¦83489 as a present to Antiokis, the¦83486 king's¦83487 concubine¦83485.
\v 31 The¦83493 king¦83494 therefore¦83492 quickly came to¦83499 settle matters, leaving¦83499 for his deputy\f + \fr 4:31 \ft Grk. \fqa successor.\f* Andronikos, a man of high rank.
\v 32 Then¦83508 Menelaos¦83510, supposing that¦83516 he had gotten a favourable opportunity, presented to Andronikos certain vessels of¦83517 gold belonging to the¦83517 temple¦83518, which he'd stolen. He'd already sold others into¦83520 Tsor \add !(Tyre)\add* and the¦83531 neighbouring cities¦83533.
\v 33 When¦83535 Honyo \add !(Onias)\add* had sure knowledge of this¦83538, he sharply reproved him, having withdrawn himself into¦83545 a sanctuary at Dafne, that¦83547 lies by Antiokeia.
\v 34 Therefore Menelaos¦83553, taking¦83554 Andronikos aside, asked him to¦83558 kill Honyo. Coming to¦83565 Honyo, and¦83568 being persuaded to¦83570 use treachery, and¦83572 being received as a friend, Andronikos gave¦83576 him his right¦83573 hand with¦83574 oaths and, though¦83577 he was suspicious, persuaded him to¦83580 come¦83564 out¦83582 of¦83583 the¦83583 sanctuary. Then¦83587, with no¦83590 regard for justice, he immediately put him to death.
\v 35 Because¦83594 this reason¦83596 not only¦83598 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, but¦83601 many also¦83602 of the¦83603 other nations¦83605, had indignation and¦83607 displeasure at the¦83610 unjust¦83613 murder¦83614 of¦83611 the¦83611 man¦83612.
\v 36 And when the king¦83617 had come back from¦83619 the¦83620 places¦83623 in Kilikia, the Yehudim who were in the city¦83627 appealed to him against Andronikos (the¦83631 Hellains \add !(Greeks)\add* also joining with¦83635 them in hatred of¦83634 the¦83634 wickedness), urging that¦83637 Honyo had been wrongfully slain.
\v 37 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* therefore¦83641 was heartily sorry, and¦83645 was moved to pity, and¦83649 wept, because¦83651 of¦83651 the sober and well ordered life of him who was dead.
\v 38 Being inflamed with anger, he immediately stripped off Andronikos's purple¦83667 robe¦83667, and tore off his under clothes, and when he'd led him round through the¦83670 whole¦83675 city¦83677 to¦83678 that¦83680 very place¦83681 where he'd committed the¦83684 outrage against Honyo, there¦83687 he put the¦83688 murderer out of¦83691 the way, the master rendering to him the punishment¦83696 he'd deserved.
\s1 The people protest against temple theft
\p
\v 39 Now when many sacrileges had been¦83698 committed in the¦83703 city¦83704 by¦83705 Lusimakos with¦83708 the¦83709 consent of Menelaos, and¦83712 when the¦83715 report of them had spread abroad outside¦83714, the¦83718 people¦83719 gathered themselves together against¦83720 Lusimakos, after many vessels of gold had already¦83724 been stolen.
\v 40 When¦83728 the¦83729 multitudes were rising against him and were filled with anger, Lusimakos armed about three thousand men, and with unrighteous violence began the¦83732 attack under the¦83736 leadership of Havran, a man¦83744 far gone in years and¦83750 no less¦83751 also¦83752 in folly.
\v 41 But¦83756 when¦83757 they perceived the¦83758 assault of Lusimakos, some caught up stones, others logs of wood¦83768, and¦83771 some took handfuls of the¦83773 ashes that¦83780 lay near, and they flung them all in wild confusion at Lusimakos and those who¦83782 were with him.
\v 42 As a result, they wounded many¦83796 of¦83784 them, they killed¦83790 some, and they forced the rest of them to¦83791 flee, but the author of the sacrilege himself they killed beside the¦83806 treasury¦83807.
\p
\v 43 But about¦83809 these matters, there was an accusation laid against Menelaos.
\v 44 When¦83818 the¦83819 king¦83820 had come to¦83823 Tsor \add !(Tyre)\add*, the¦83825 three¦83831 men¦83830 who¦83828 were sent¦83829 by¦83832 the senate pleaded the cause before him.
\v 45 But¦83836 Menelaos¦83839, seeing himself now defeated, promised much money¦83841 to¦83847 Ptolemaios \add !(Ptolemy)\add* the¦83848 son of Dorumenes, that he might win over the¦83850 king¦83851.
\v 46 Therefore Ptolemaios¦83855 taking the¦83861 king¦83862 aside into a cloister, as if to get some fresh air, convinced him to change his mind.
\v 47 He who¦83865 was the¦83867 cause of all¦83868 the evil¦83869, Menelaos, he discharged from the accusations; but¦83876 these hapless men, who¦83878, if they'd pleaded even before Scythians, would have been discharged uncondemned, them he sentenced to death¦83888.
\v 48 Those who were spokesmen for¦83897 the city¦83898 and¦83899 the families of Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* and¦83901 the¦83902 holy vessels soon¦83890 suffered that unrighteous penalty.
\v 49 Therefore even certain Tsorim \add !(Tyrians)\add*, moved with¦83913 hatred of¦83906 the wickedness, provided magnificently for their burial.
\v 50 But¦83920 Menelaos¦83921, through¦83922 the covetous dealings of¦83922 those who were in power, remained still in his office, growing in wickedness¦83933, established as a great¦83934 conspirator against¦83928 his fellow-citizens.
\c 5
\s1 Yason attacks Yerushalem
\p
\v 1 Now about¦83939 this¦83941 time¦83942 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* made his second¦83945 invasion into Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add*.
\v 2 It happened¦83952 that throughout all¦83955 the¦83956 city¦83957, for almost forty¦83961 days¦83960, cavalry appeared in the middle of the¦83964 sky in swift motion, wearing robes¦83969 woven with gold and¦83971 carrying spears, equipped with troops for battle—
\v 3 drawing swords¦83992, squadrons of cavalry in array, encounters and pursuits of both armies, shaking shields, multitudes¦83990 of lances, throwing of missiles, flashing of golden¦83998 trappings, and¦84001 putting on all sorts of armour.
\v 4 Therefore everyone¦84005 prayed that the¦84009 manifestation might have been¦84011 given for good.
\p
\v 5 When a false rumour had arisen that Antiokos was dead, Yason¦84023 took not less than a thousand¦84027 men, and suddenly made an assault on¦84029 the¦84030 city¦84031. When those who¦84034 were on¦84036 the¦84037 wall were being routed, and¦84040 the¦84044 city¦84045 was at length nearly taken, Menelaos¦84047 took refuge in the¦84049 citadel.
\v 6 But¦84053 Yason¦84054 slaughtered his own¦84060 citizens¦84058 without mercy, not considering that¦84064 good success against kinsmen is the¦84066 greatest misfortune, but supposing himself to¦84070 be¦84070 setting up trophies over enemies, and not over fellow-countrymen.
\v 7 He didn't win control of the government, but¦84087 receiving shame as the¦84089 result of his conspiracy, he fled again¦84094 as a fugitive into the country of the Amonim.
\v 8 At last therefore¦84100 he met with¦84105 a miserable end¦84099. Having been imprisoned at the court of Aretas the prince of the Aravim, fleeing from¦84112 city to city, pursued by¦84116 all¦84117 men, hated as an rebel against the¦84121 laws, and¦84124 abhorred¦84125 as the executioner of his country and¦84128 his fellow citizens¦84129, he was thrown ashore in Mitsrayim.
\v 9 He who'd¦84135 driven many from their own country into exile perished in exile, having crossed the¦84137 sea to the Spartans, hoping to find shelter¦84151 there because¦84147 they were\f + \fr 5:9 \ft See 1 Maccabees 12:7.\f* near of¦84147 kin.
\v 10 He who'd¦84153 thrown out a multitude¦84154 unburied had none to¦84158 mourn for him. He didn't¦84161 have any funeral at all and no place in the tomb¦84165 of his ancestors¦84164.
\s1 Antiokos plunders Yerushalem and the temple
\p
\v 11 Now when¦84168 news came to the¦84169 king¦84170 about¦84171 that¦84172 which was done, he thought that¦84176 Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add* was in revolt. So, setting out¦84180 from¦84180 Mitsrayim in a rage, he took¦84185 the¦84186 city¦84188 by force of weapons,
\v 12 and¦84190 commanded his soldiers¦84193 to¦84191 cut down without mercy those who came in their way, and¦84198 to kill those who went into¦84203 their houses.
\v 13 Then there was killing of young and old, destruction of boys, women¦84214, and¦84215 children¦84216, and¦84219 slaying of virgins¦84218 and¦84220 infants.
\v 14 In¦84226 a total¦84228 of three¦84230 days¦84229, eighty thousand were destroyed, of which forty thousand were slain in¦84234 close combat, and no¦84237 fewer were sold into¦84241 slavery than slain.
\p
\v 15 Not content with this, he presumed to¦84256 enter¦84256 into the¦84250 most holy temple¦84255 of all¦84251 the¦84252 earth¦84253, having Menelaos for his guide¦84257 (who'd proved himself a traitor both to¦84258 the laws and to his country),
\v 16 even taking¦84277 the¦84274 sacred¦84275 vessels¦84276 with his polluted hands, and¦84278 dragging down with his profane¦84293 hands the¦84279 offerings that'd been dedicated by¦84280 other kings¦84282 to¦84284 enhance the glory¦84287 and¦84286 honour¦84291 of¦84288 the¦84288 place¦84289.
\v 17 Antiokos was lifted up in mind¦84300, not seeing that¦84305 because¦84306 of¦84306 the¦84307 sins¦84308 of those who¦84309 lived in the¦84310 city¦84311 the¦84315 Sovereign master¦84316 had been provoked to¦84318 anger a little while, and therefore his eye was¦84318 turned away from the¦84320 place¦84321.
\v 18 But¦84324 had it not¦84325 been so that they were already bound by many sins, this man, even as Heliodoros who was sent by¦84335 King¦84338 Seleukos \add !(Seleucus)\add* to¦84339 view the¦84342 treasury¦84343, would, as soon as he¦84344 came forward, have been scourged and turned back from his daring deed.
\v 19 However the¦84354 master didn't choose the¦84356 nation¦84357 for the¦84360 place's sake, but the¦84362 place¦84363 for the¦84364 nation's sake.
\v 20 Therefore¦84367 also¦84368 the¦84370 place¦84371 itself, having shared in the¦84373 calamities that happened to the¦84374 nation¦84375, did afterward share in its benefits; and¦84384 the¦84385 place which was forsaken in¦84387 the¦84389 Almighty's¦84390 anger¦84391 was, at the¦84394 reconciliation¦84398 of¦84395 the¦84395 great¦84396 Sovereign, restored again¦84392 with¦84399 all¦84400 glory¦84401.
\p
\v 21 As for Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, when he'd carried away out¦84411 of¦84412 the¦84412 temple¦84413 one thousand and eight hundred talents¦84414, he hurried away to Antiokeia, thinking in his arrogance that he could sail on¦84424 land¦84425 and¦84427 walk on the¦84428 sea¦84429, because¦84432 his heart¦84436 was lifted up.
\v 22 Moreover he left¦84437 governors to¦84437 afflict the race: at Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, Filippos \add !(Philip)\add*, by race a Phrygian, and in¦84445 character more barbarous than him who set him there;
\v 23 and¦84461 at Gerizim, Andronikos; and¦84465 besides these, Menelaos, who¦84470 worse than all the¦84473 rest, exalted himself against his fellow-citizens. Having a malicious mind\f + \fr 5:23 \ft Some authorities read \fqa towards the Jews, he sent. \ft The Greek text of this sentence is uncertain.\f* towards the¦84478 Yehudim\f + \fr 5:23 \ft Compare 2 Maccabees 4:9, 19; 9:19.\f* whom he'd¦84481 made his citizens,
\v 24 he sent¦84483 that¦84485\f + \fr 5:24 \ft Grk. \fqa Μυσάρχην, \ft which also may mean \fqa ruler of the Mysians.\f* lord of pollutions Apolonios with¦84488 an army of twenty-two thousand, commanding him to¦84491 kill all¦84498 those who were of full age¦84497, and to sell the women¦84502 and¦84503 the boys as slaves.
\v 25 He¦84506 came¦84508 to¦84508 Yerushalem, and¦84511 pretending to¦84515 be a man of peace, waited till¦84516 the¦84517 holy¦84518 day¦84519 of¦84520 the¦84520 Sabbath¦84521, and¦84522 finding the Yehudim at rest from work, he commanded his men to¦84531 parade fully armed.
\v 26 He put to the¦84533 sword all¦84535 those who came out to¦84536 the¦84537 spectacle. Running into the¦84542 city¦84543 with¦84544 the¦84545 armed men, he killed great multitudes¦84550.
\v 27 But¦84552 Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add*, who's¦84553 also¦84558 called Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, with about nine others, withdrew himself¦84572, and¦84573 with¦84579 his company kept himself alive in the¦84580 mountains¦84567 like wild animals¦84563 do. They continued feeding on¦84565 what grew wild, that they might not¦84581 be partakers of¦84583 the¦84583 defilement¦84584.
\c 6
\s1 The king forbids obedience to God's law
\p
\v 1 Not long after¦84585 this, the¦84591 king¦84592 sent¦84590 out¦84590 an\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Or, \fqa Geron an Athenian\f* old man of Athainai to compel the¦84596 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* to depart from¦84599 the¦84600 laws of their fathers and¦84603 not to live by God's¦84606 laws,
\v 2 and also to pollute the¦84613 sanctuary in¦84614 Yerushalem and¦84617 to call it by the name of Olympian Zeus, and¦84621 to call the¦84622 sanctuary in¦84623 Gerizim by the name of Zeus the Protector of foreigners, even as¦84625 the¦84627 people who¦84628 lived in that place¦84629 did.
\p
\v 3 The visitation of this evil¦84644 was harsh and utterly grievous.
\v 4 Because the¦84645 temple¦84648 was filled with debauchery¦84649 and¦84650 revelling by¦84653 the heathen, who dallied with¦84657 prostitutes,\f + \fr 6:4 \ft Or, \fqa idled with their fellows\f* and¦84659 had intercourse with women within the¦84661 sacred precincts, and¦84667 moreover brought inside¦84671 things that¦84668 weren't¦84669 appropriate.
\v 5 The¦84673 altar¦84675 was filled with those abominable things which had been prohibited by the laws.
\v 6 A man could neither keep the Sabbath, nor observe the feasts of their ancestors, nor so¦84684 much as confess himself to¦84695 be¦84695 a Yehudi \add !(Jew)\add*.
\p
\v 7 On the¦84702 day¦84708 of¦84705 the¦84705 king's¦84706 birth every month¦84704, they were led along with bitter constraint to eat of the sacrifices. When¦84712 the¦84716 feast of Dionusia came, they were compelled to¦84719 go in procession in honour of Dionusos, wearing wreaths of ivy.
\v 8 A¦84723 decree went out¦84725 to the¦84727 neighbouring Greek cities¦84729, by the suggestion of Ptolemaios \add !(Ptolemy)\add*, that they should observe the same conduct against the Yehudim, and¦84740 should make them eat of the sacrifices,
\v 9 and¦84743 that they should kill those who didn't¦84744 choose to go over to the¦84748 Greek rites. So¦84752 the¦84754 present¦84755 misery was for all to see.
\v 10 Because example, two¦84757 women¦84759 were brought in for¦84758 having circumcised their children¦84763. These, when they'd led them publicly¦84774 around the¦84777 city¦84778 with the babes hung¦84770 from their breasts, they threw down headlong from the¦84780 wall¦84781.
\v 11 Others¦84783 who'd run together into the caves¦84789 nearby¦84785 to keep the¦84788 seventh day secretly¦84790, were betrayed to¦84791 Filippos \add !(Philip)\add* and were all burnt together, because¦84798 their piety kept them from defending themselves, in view of¦84798 the honour¦84806 of that¦84807 most solemn day¦84809.
\s1 God's discipline and mercy
\p
\v 12 I urge those who¦84812 read this book¦84816 to not¦84817 be discouraged because¦84819 of¦84819 the¦84820 calamities, but¦84823 recognise that¦84824 these punishments¦84825 weren't¦84826 for the destruction¦84828, but¦84829 for the chastening of our race.
\v 13 Because indeed it's a sign¦84850 of great¦84848 kindness that those who¦84837 act impiously aren't¦84838 let alone for a long time¦84840, but¦84844 immediately¦84845 meet with retribution.
\v 14 Because¦84853 in the case of the other nations¦84859, the¦84862 Sovereign master¦84863 waits patiently¦84861 to punish them until¦84864 they've attained to the full measure of their sins¦84870; but¦84873 not with us,
\v 15 that¦84878 he may not¦84879 take vengeance on us afterward,\f + \fr 6:15 \ft Or, \fqa when our sins have come to their height\f* when we've come to¦84880 the¦84884\f + \fr 6:15 \ft Grk. \fqa end.\f* height of our sins¦84885.
\v 16 Therefore¦84889 he never withdraws his mercy¦84894 from¦84896 us; but¦84900 though he chastens with¦84901 calamity, he doesn't forsake his own people¦84907.
\v 17 However¦84908 let this that we've¦84912 spoken suffice to remind you; but after a few¦84915 words, we must¦84917 come¦84917 to¦84918 the¦84919 narrative¦84920.
\s1 Eleazar chooses death over disobedience
\p
\v 18 Eleazar, one¦84922 of the¦84923 principal scribes¦84925, a man¦84926 already¦84927 well advanced in years, and¦84931 of¦84934 a noble face¦84935, was compelled to open his mouth to¦84940 eat¦84940 swine's flesh.
\v 19 But¦84944 he, welcoming death¦84948 with honour rather than¦84950 life¦84954 with defilement, advanced of his own accord to the instrument of torture, but first spat out the flesh,
\v 20 as men ought to¦84967 come¦84967 who¦84968 are resolute to¦84974 repel such things as not even for¦84975 the natural love of¦84975 life¦84979 is it lawful to taste¦84974.
\p
\v 21 But those who had the charge¦84987 of¦84988 that forbidden sacrificial feast took the¦84989 man¦84996 aside, for the¦84991 acquaintance which of old times¦84993 they had with¦84994 him, and privately implored him to bring flesh of his own providing, such as was proper for him to use, and¦85012 to¦85014 make as if he did eat¦85014 of¦85017 the¦85020 flesh from¦85021 the¦85022 sacrifice¦85023, as had been commanded by the¦85017 king¦85018;
\v 22 that¦85025 by so doing¦85027 he¦85026 might be delivered from death¦85030, and¦85031 so his ancient friendship with¦85035 them might be treated kindly.
\v 23 But¦85041 he, having formed a high resolve, and¦85045 one that¦85047 became his years, the¦85050 dignity of¦85051 old age¦85048, and¦85054 the¦85055 grey hairs\f + \fr 6:23 \ft The Greek text appears to be corrupt.\f* which he'd reached with honour, and his excellent\f + \fr 6:23 \ft Some authorities read \fqa manner of life.\f* education from¦85062 a child¦85063, or rather the¦85068 holy¦85069 laws\f + \fr 6:23 \ft Grk. \fqa legislation.\f* of God's ordaining, declared his mind accordingly, bidding them to quickly¦85075 send him to¦85076 Haidais.
\p
\v 24 “Because¦85082 it doesn't become our¦85084 years to¦85087 dissemble,” he said, “that¦85089 many of the¦85091 young should suppose that Eleazar, the¦85095 man of ninety years, had gone over to an alien religion;
\v 25 and¦85100 so they, because¦85102 of¦85102 my¦85104 deception, and¦85106 for¦85107 the sake of¦85107 this¦85108 brief and¦85110 momentary life¦85112, would be led astray because¦85114 of¦85114 me, and I¦85115 defile and disgrace myself in my old age.
\v 26 Because even if¦85123 for¦85124 the¦85127 present¦85128 time I'd remove from¦85131 me the¦85135 punishment of¦85136 men, yet whether I live¦85140 or die¦85142, I wouldn't escape the¦85136 Almighty's¦85137 hands¦85138.
\v 27 Therefore¦85144, by bravely parting with my life¦85150 now¦85147, I'll show myself worthy¦85154 of my old age,
\v 28 and¦85157 leave\f + \fr 6:28 \ft Grk. \fqa one that has left behind.\f* behind a noble example¦85159 to the¦85163 young to die willingly and¦85165 nobly a glorious death for¦85167 the¦85168 revered and¦85170 holy¦85171 laws.”
\p When¦85175 he'd said¦85176 these words, he went¦85181 immediately¦85180 to¦85181 the instrument of torture.
\v 29 When¦85183 they changed the¦85185 good will they bore towards¦85191 him a little¦85186 before¦85187 into¦85189 ill will because¦85194 these words¦85198 of¦85194 his were¦85203, as they thought, sheer madness,\f + \fr 6:29 \ft The Greek text of this verse is uncertain.\f*
\v 30 and¦85205 when he was at the¦85206 point to die with the\f + \fr 6:30 \ft Or, \fqa blows\f* blows¦85207, he groaned aloud and said¦85210, “To the¦85211 master, who has the¦85214 holy¦85215 knowledge¦85216, it's manifest¦85218 that¦85220, while I might have been delivered from death¦85224, I endure¦85226 severe pains in my body¦85229 by being scourged; but in soul¦85233 I gladly¦85235 suffer¦85241 these things because¦85236 of¦85236 my¦85238 fear¦85239 of him.”
\p
\v 31 So this man also died like this¦85259, leaving¦85267 his death¦85261 for an example¦85262 of nobleness and¦85264 a memorial¦85265 of virtue, not only¦85250 to the¦85251 young but¦85253 also to the¦85255 great body of¦85257 his nation¦85258.
\c 7
\s1 Seven brothers die for God's law
\p
\v 1 It happened¦85268 that seven¦85271 brothers¦85272 and¦85269 their mother were at the¦85274 king's¦85280 command taken and¦85270 shamefully handled with¦85273 scourges and cords, to compel them to taste of¦85279 the abominable swine's flesh.
\v 2 One¦85291 of them made himself the spokesman and¦85292 said¦85297, “What'd you ask¦85300 and¦85301 learn from¦85303 us? Because we're¦85304 ready¦85305 to¦85307 die¦85307 rather than¦85309 transgress the¦85311 laws¦85313 of our ancestors.”
\p
\v 3 The¦85317 king¦85318 fell into¦85319 a rage, and commanded that pans and cauldrons be heated.
\v 4 When¦85325 these were immediately heated, he¦85330 gave orders¦85332 to cut out the¦85324 tongue of him who'd¦85328 been¦85329 their spokesman, and to scalp him, and¦85334 to cut off his extremities, with the¦85337 rest of his brothers¦85339 and¦85340 his mother looking on.
\v 5 And¦85345 when he¦85346 was utterly\f + \fr 7:5 \ft Grk. \fqa useless.\f* maimed, the¦85347 king gave orders¦85350 to¦85349 bring¦85353 him to¦85350 the¦85351 fire¦85352, being yet alive, and¦85355 to fry him in the¦85357 pan. And¦85358 as the smoke from the pan spread far, they and their mother¦85369 also exhorted one another¦85365 to¦85366 die nobly, saying this¦85368:
\v 6 “The master God¦85377 sees, and¦85379 in truth is\f + \fr 7:6 \ft Or, \fqa comforted in\f* entreated for us, as¦85385 Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add* declared in his\f + \fr 7:6 \ft See Deuteronomy 31:21 and 32:36.\f* song, which witnesses against the¦85387 people to¦85388 their faces¦85389, saying, ‘And¦85395 he'll¦85399 have compassion on his servants.’ ”
\p
\v 7 And¦85402 when the¦85403 first¦85404 had died like this, they brought the¦85408 second¦85409 to¦85411 the¦85412 mocking; and¦85414 they pulled off the skin of his head¦85417 with¦85419 the¦85420 hair and asked him, “Will you eat¦85425, before¦85426 your body¦85430 is punished in every limb?”
\p
\v 8 But he answered in the language of his ancestors and said to them, “No.” Therefore he also underwent the next torture in succession, as the first had done.
\v 9 When he was at the last gasp, he said, “You, miscreant, release us out of this present life, but the King of the world will raise us who've died for his laws up to an eternal renewal of life.”
\p
\v 10 After¦85483 him, the¦85486 third¦85487 was made a victim of their mocking. When¦85489 he was required, he quickly¦85493 put out his tongue¦85491, and¦85495 stretched out his hands¦85497 courageously,
\v 11 and¦85500 nobly said¦85502, “I got these from¦85503 heaven¦85504. Because¦85508 his laws¦85511’ sake I treat these with¦85515 contempt. From him, I hope¦85519 to receive these back again¦85518.”
\v 12 As a result, the¦85523 king¦85524 himself and¦85525 those who were with¦85527 him were¦85529 astonished¦85529 at the young man's soul¦85533, for he regarded the¦85537 pains as nothing.
\p
\v 13 When¦85540 he¦85541 too was dead, they shamefully handled and¦85542 tortured the¦85544 fourth¦85545 in the same way.
\v 14 Being near to¦85551 death he said¦85555 this¦85552: “It's good to¦85553 die at the¦85560 hands¦85571 of¦85562 men and look for the¦85562 hope which is given by God¦85563, that we'll be raised up again¦85566 by¦85568 him. Because as for you¦85570, you'll have no¦85576 resurrection¦85573 to life¦85575.”
\p
\v 15 Next after him, they brought the¦85580 fifth and¦85579 shamefully handled him.
\v 16 But¦85585 he¦85587 looked towards the\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Grk. \fqa him.\f* king and said¦85589, “Because you have authority¦85590 among¦85591 men, though you're corruptible, you do¦85598 what you please. But don't¦85599 think¦85600 that¦85602 our race has been forsaken by¦85605 God¦85607.
\v 17 But¦85610 hold on to¦85613 your ways, and see¦85613 how¦85618 his sovereign majesty will torture you¦85619 and¦85620 your descendants!”
\p
\v 18 After¦85625 him they brought the sixth. When he was about¦85640 to die¦85633, he said¦85634, “Don't¦85635 be vainly deceived, for we¦85638 suffer¦85643 these things for our own doings, as sinning¦85644 against our own God¦85648. Astounding things have happen;
\v 19 but¦85654 don't¦85655 think you that you'll be¦85658 unpunished, having tried to¦85658 fight against God!”
\s1 A mother encourages her sons to remain faithful
\p
\v 20 But¦85662 above all, the¦85663 mother¦85664 was marvellous¦85665 and¦85666 worthy¦85669 of honourable memory; for when she watched seven¦85673 sons¦85672 perishing within the space of one¦85675 day¦85678, she bore¦85680 the sight with a good courage because¦85681 of¦85681 her hope in the¦85682 master.
\v 21 She exhorted each¦85686 one of them in the¦85690 language of their fathers, filled with a noble spirit and¦85696 stirring up her woman's thoughts with manly courage, saying to them,
\v 22 “I don't¦85706 know how you came into my¦85711 womb¦85713. It wasn't I¦85715 who¦85716 gave you your spirit¦85717\f + \fr 7:22 \ft Or, \fqa breath\f* and¦85718 your life¦85720. It wasn't I who brought into order the first elements of each¦85725 one of you.
\v 23 Therefore¦85730 the¦85731 Creator¦85734 of¦85732 the¦85732 world¦85733, who shaped the first origin of man¦85737 and¦85739 devised the first origin of all¦85740 things, in mercy¦85753 gives back to you again¦85750 both your spirit¦85745\f + \fr 7:23 \ft Or, \fqa breath\f* and¦85746 your life¦85748, as you¦85749 now¦85755 treat yourselves with contempt for¦85758 his laws’ sake.”
\p
\v 24 But¦85762 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, thinking himself to be despised, and¦85766 suspecting the¦85767 reproachful voice¦85770, while the¦85772 youngest was yet alive didn't only¦85776 make his appeal to him by words¦85778, but¦85782 also¦85783 at the same time promised with oaths that he'd enrich him and¦85789\f + \fr 7:24 \ft Grk. \fqa make him one that is counted happy.\f* raise him to high honour if he'd turn from¦85793 the¦85794 ways of his ancestors, and¦85797 that he'd take him for his friend¦85798\f + \fr 7:24 \ft See 2 Maccabees 8:9.\f* and¦85800 entrust him with public affairs.
\v 25 But when the young¦85805 man¦85805 would in no way listen, the¦85809 king¦85810 called¦85808 to¦85808 him his mother¦85812, and urged¦85813 her to¦85813 advise the¦85814 youth to save himself.
\v 26 When¦85821 he'd urged her with many words, she¦85822 undertook to persuade her son¦85827.
\v 27 But¦85829 bending towards¦85860 him, laughing the cruel tyrant¦85834 to scorn, she spoke this in the language of her fathers: “My son¦85840, have¦85841 pity on me who¦85843 carried you¦85847 nine¦85849 months¦85848 in my womb¦85845, and¦85850 nursed you¦85852 three¦85854 years¦85853, and nourished and brought you up to this¦85861 age¦85862, and¦85864 sustained you.
\v 28 I beg you¦85867, my child¦85868, to lift your eyes to the¦85871 sky¦85872 and¦85873 the¦85874 earth¦85875, and¦85876 to see all¦85880 things that are in¦85878 it¦85879, and thus to¦85882 recognise¦85882 that¦85883 God¦85890 made¦85887 them not of things that were, and¦85891 that the¦85892 race of men in this¦85893 way comes into being.
\v 29 Don't¦85898 be afraid¦85899 of this butcher, but¦85903, proving yourself worthy¦85906 of your brothers, accept your death¦85910, that¦85911 in¦85912 God's mercy¦85914 I may receive you again with¦85915 your brothers¦85917.”
\p
\v 30 But¦85922 before she'd¦85923 finished speaking, the¦85925 young¦85926 man¦85926 said¦85927, “What are you all waiting for? I don't obey¦85931 the¦85934 king's¦85935 commandment, but¦85937 I listen¦85939 to¦85939 the commandment of¦85940 the¦85940 law¦85941 that was given¦85943 to¦85943 our fathers¦85945 through¦85947 Mosheh \add !(Moses)\add*.
\v 31 But¦85950 you¦85949, who've devised all¦85951 kinds of evil¦85952 against the¦85956 Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*, will in no¦85958 way escape God's¦85964 hands¦85962.
\v 32 Because we're¦85965 suffering¦85971 because¦85967 of¦85967 our own sins¦85970.
\v 33 If¦85972 for¦85974 rebuke and¦85976 chastening, our living¦85979 master has been angered a little while, yet he'll again¦85985 be reconciled with his own servants.
\v 34 But you¦85990, Oh¦85992 unholy man¦85996 and¦85994 of all¦85995 most vile, don't¦85998 be vainly lifted up in¦85999 your wild pride with uncertain hopes, raising your hand¦86009 against¦86004 the¦86005 heavenly children.
\v 35 Because you haven't¦86010 yet¦86010 escaped the¦86012 judgement¦86017 of¦86013 the¦86013 Almighty¦86014 God¦86016 who sees all things.
\v 36 Because¦86021 these our¦86023 brothers¦86024, having endured a short pain that brings eternal life¦86029,\f + \fr 7:36 \ft Grk. \fqa short pain of ever-flowing life.\f* have now¦86022 died under¦86030 God's¦86032 covenant¦86031.\f + \fr 7:36 \ft Grk. \fqa died. \ft By the omission of one Greek letter the words would signify \fqa having endured a short pain, have now drunk of ever-flowing life under God's covenant.\f* But¦86035 you¦86034, through God's¦86038 judgement¦86039, will receive in just¦86040 measure the penalties of your arrogance.
\v 37 But I, as¦86048 my brothers¦86050, give¦86056 up both body¦86053 and¦86054 soul¦86055 for the¦86058 laws of our fathers, calling¦86061 on¦86061 God¦86063 that he may speedily become gracious\f + \fr 7:37 \ft Grk. \fqa propitious.\f* to the¦86066 nation¦86067, and¦86069 that you¦86070, amidst trials and¦86073 \add ≈deadly diseases\add*, may confess that he alone¦86077 is God¦86079,
\v 38 and that in¦86081 me and my¦86082 brothers¦86086 you may bring to¦86088 an end\f + \fr 7:38 \ft Some authorities read \fqa may be stayed.\f* the¦86090 Almighty's¦86091 anger¦86092 which has been justly brought on¦86094 our whole race.”
\p
\v 39 But¦86102 the¦86104 king¦86105, falling into a rage, handled him worse than all the rest, being exasperated at his mocking.
\v 40 So¦86117 he¦86118 also¦86117 died pure, putting his whole trust in the master.
\p
\v 41 Last¦86129 of all, after her sons¦86132, the¦86133 mother¦86134 died.
\p
\v 42 Let it then¦86138 suffice to¦86145 have said thus much about¦86139 the sacrificial feasts and¦86141 the¦86142 extreme tortures.
\c 8
\s1 Yehudah Makabi gathers a resistance force
\p
\v 1 But¦86149 Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add*, who's¦86150 also called Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, and¦86152 those who¦86153 were with¦86154 him, making their way secretly¦86157 into the¦86159 villages¦86160, called to them their kindred¦86163. Taking¦86170 to them those who'd¦86165 continued in¦86167 the¦86168 Yehudim’ \add !(Jews)\add* religion, gathered together about six thousand.
\v 2 They called on the¦86176 master to¦86179 look at the people who were oppressed by¦86181 all¦86182, and to have compassion on the¦86188 sanctuary that'd¦86190 been profaned by¦86191 the¦86192 ungodly¦86193 men,
\v 3 and to¦86196 have¦86196 pity on the¦86199 city¦86201 that was suffering ruin and¦86202 ready to be¦86205 levelled to the ground, and to listen to the blood¦86211 that cried out to him,
\v 4 and to¦86213 remember¦86213 the lawless destruction¦86221 of the innocent infants, and¦86222 about¦86223 the¦86224 blasphemies that'd been¦86225 committed against his name¦86228, and¦86231 to show his hatred of wickedness.
\p
\v 5 When¦86234 Makabi had trained his men for service, the heathen at once found him irresistible, for the¦86246 master's anger¦86245 was turned into mercy¦86249.
\v 6 Coming¦86256\f + \fr 8:6 \ft The Greek text of verses 6 and 7 is uncertain.\f* without warning, he set fire to cities¦86251 and villages¦86254. And¦86258 in winning back the¦86259 most important positions, putting to flight no¦86263 small number of the¦86265 enemies,
\v 7 he especially¦86269 took advantage of the¦86270 nights¦86271 for such¦86274 assaults. His courage was loudly talked of everywhere¦86285.
\s1 Yehudah defeats Nikanor's army
\p
\v 8 But¦86287 when Filippos¦86289 \add !(Philip)\add* saw the¦86295 man¦86296 gaining ground little by little, and increasing more and more in¦86299 his success, he wrote¦86311 to¦86303 Ptolemaios \add !(Ptolemy)\add*, the¦86305 governor of Coele-Syria and¦86308 Foinikai, that¦86313 he should support the¦86314 king's¦86315 cause.
\v 9 Ptolemaios quickly¦86319 appointed Nikanor the¦86322 son of Patroklos, one of the king's chief friends,\f + \fr 8:9 \ft See 1 Maccabees 10:65. Compare 2 Maccabees 1:14; 7:24; 10:13; 14:11; 1 Maccabees 2:18.\f* and sent¦86328 him, in command of no¦86332 fewer than twenty thousand of all nations¦86331, to destroy the whole race of Yehudah. With him he¦86344 joined Gorgias also, a captain and one who had¦86354 experience in¦86350 matters of war.
\v 10 Nikanor¦86358 resolved by the sale of the captive Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* to make up for the¦86361 king¦86362 the¦86363 tribute of two thousand talents¦86366 which he was to pay to the Romaios.
\v 11 Immediately¦86374 he sent¦86380 to the¦86377 cities¦86379 on the sea coast, inviting them to¦86382 buy Jewish slaves,\f + \fr 8:11 \ft Grk. \fqa bodies.\f* promising to¦86392 deliver seventy slaves\f + \fr 8:11 \ft Grk. \fqa bodies.\f* for¦86392 a talent, not expecting¦86392 the¦86393 judgement that was to overtake him from¦86394 the¦86395 Almighty¦86396.
\p
\v 12 News came¦86417 to Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* about¦86406 Nikanor's invasion. When¦86411 he¦86413 communicated to those who were with¦86414 him the¦86416 presence of¦86418 the¦86418 army,
\v 13 those who¦86420 were cowardly and¦86423 distrustful of¦86426 God's¦86427 judgement ran\f + \fr 8:13 \ft The Greek text here is uncertain.\f* away and¦86430 left the country.
\v 14 Others sold all¦86437 that¦86433 they'd left, and¦86434 at the same time implored the master to¦86438 deliver those who'd been sold¦86438 as slaves by¦86446 the¦86447 impious Nikanor before¦86450 he ever met¦86451 them,
\v 15 if¦86454 not¦86455 for¦86456 their own sakes, then for¦86459 the¦86460 covenants made with¦86461 their ancestors, and¦86466 because¦86467 he'd¦86470 called them by his holy and¦86474 glorious name¦86476.
\v 16 So Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* gathered his men together, six thousand in number¦86487, and¦86479 exhorted them not¦86490 to¦86485 be¦86485 frightened by the¦86486 enemy, nor¦86494 to¦86489 fear the¦86492 great multitude of the¦86496 heathen who¦86497 came wrongfully against¦86500 them, but¦86505 to fight nobly,
\v 17 setting before¦86507 their eyes¦86508 the¦86510 outrage that'd been lawlessly perpetrated on the¦86513 holy¦86514 place¦86515, and¦86520 the¦86521 torture of the¦86522 city¦86524 that'd been turned to mockery, and¦86527 further the¦86528 overthrow of the¦86529 way of life received from their ancestors.
\v 18 “Because¦86535 they,” he said, “trust their weapons and¦86539 daring actions, but we trust in the¦86545 almighty God¦86547, since he's¦86548 able¦86548 at a nod to throw down those who are coming¦86551 against¦86552 us, and¦86554 even the¦86555 whole¦86556 world¦86557.”
\v 19 Moreover, he¦86565 recounted to¦86568 them the¦86569 help¦86572 given from time to time in the days of their ancestors¦86570, both in the days of Sanheriv \add !(Sennacherib)\add*, when one hundred¦86578 and eighty-five thousand¦86581 perished,
\v 20 and¦86584 in the¦86585 land of Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add*, in¦86586 the¦86587 battle that¦86589 was¦86594 fought against¦86598 the¦86599\f + \fr 8:20 \ft Grk. \fqa Galatians.\f* Gauls, how they came¦86601 to the battle with eight¦86602 thousand¦86602 in all¦86597, with¦86603 four thousand people from Makedonia, and how, the¦86606 people from Makedonia being hard pressed, the¦86609 six\f + \fr 8:20 \ft Some authorities read \fqa eight.\f* thousand¦86610 destroyed the¦86611 hundred and twenty thousand because¦86615 of¦86615 the¦86616 help¦86621 which they had from¦86619 heaven¦86620, and¦86622 took¦86625 a great deal of plunder.
\p
\v 21 And when he¦86629 had with these words filled them with courage and¦86631 made¦86644 them ready¦86632 to¦86639 die¦86639 for the¦86642 laws and their country, he divided his army into four parts.
\v 22 He appointed his brothers¦86648, Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add*, Yosef \add !(Joseph)\add*, and Yonatan \add !(Jonathan)\add*, to be leaders of the divisions with him, giving each¦86659 the¦86662 command of one thousand and five¦86663 hundred¦86663 men.
\v 23 Moreover Eleazar also, having read aloud the¦86669 sacred book, and¦86672 having given¦86673 as watchword, “THE HELP OF GOD¦86675”, leading the¦86677 first¦86678 band himself, joined battle with Nikanor.
\p
\v 24 Since the¦86688 Almighty¦86689 fought on their side, they killed¦86697 more than nine thousand of the enemy, and¦86698 wounded and¦86699\f + \fr 8:24 \ft Grk. \fqa disabled in their limbs.\f* disabled most¦86704 of¦86707 Nikanor's army¦86709, and compelled them all¦86711 to¦86713 flee¦86713.
\v 25 They took¦86724 the¦86715 money¦86717 of those who'd come there to buy them as slaves. After they'd pursued them for some distance,\f + \fr 8:25 \ft Or, \fqa while\f* they returned, being constrained by¦86731 the¦86732 time¦86733 of the day;
\v 26 for¦86736 it was¦86735 the¦86737 day before¦86738 the¦86739 Sabbath¦86740, and for¦86741 this reason¦86743 they made no¦86744 effort to chase them far.
\v 27 When they'd gathered the¦86752 weapons of the¦86755 enemy\f + \fr 8:27 \ft Grk. \fqa their weapons…the spoils of the enemy.\f* together,\f + \fr 8:27 \ft The exact meaning of this clause is uncertain.\f* and had stripped¦86754 off their spoils, they kept the¦86758 Sabbath¦86759, greatly blessing¦86762 and¦86763 thanking the¦86765 master who'd¦86767 saved them to this¦86771 day¦86772, because he'd¦86773 begun¦86774 to show mercy¦86775 to them.
\v 28 After¦86778 the¦86780 Sabbath¦86781, when they'd given some of the spoils to the maimed,\f + \fr 8:28 \ft Or, \fqa wounded \ft Grk. \fqa shamefully handled.\f* and¦86784 to the¦86785 widows¦86786 and¦86787 orphans, they distributed the rest among themselves and¦86796 their children¦86798.
\v 29 When¦86801 they'd accomplished these things and¦86803 had made¦86806 a common¦86804 supplication, they implored the merciful master to be wholly reconciled with his servants.
\s1 Further victories and Nikanor's escape
\p
\v 30 Having had an encounter with the¦86818 forces of Timotheos and¦86821 Bakides, they killed more than twenty thousand of them, and¦86829 made¦86853 themselves masters of extremely high strongholds¦86830, and divided very much plunder, giving the¦86843 maimed,\f + \fr 8:30 \ft Or, \fqa wounded \ft Grk. \fqa shamefully handled.\f* orphans, widows¦86848, and the aged an equal share with themselves.
\v 31 When¦86855 they'd gathered the weapons of the enemy\f + \fr 8:31 \ft Grk. \fqa of them.\f* together,\f + \fr 8:31 \ft The exact meaning of this clause is uncertain.\f* they stored them all¦86858 up carefully¦86857 in the most strategic positions, and they carried the rest of the spoils to¦86869 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 32 They killed the¦86872 phylarch\f + \fr 8:32 \ft That is, probably, the captain of an irregular auxiliary force. Some write \fqa Phylarches, \ft as a proper name.\f* of Timotheos's forces, a most unholy man¦86880, and¦86873 one who'd¦86875 done¦86878 the Yehudim much¦86882 harm.
\v 33 As they celebrated the feast of victory in¦86889 the¦86890 city\f + \fr 8:33 \ft Or, \fqa country\f* of their fathers, they burnt those who'd¦86892 set the¦86894 sacred gates¦86896\f + \fr 8:33 \ft Or, \fqa porches\f* on¦86903 fire, including Kalisthenes, who'd¦86906 fled into a\f + \fr 8:33 \ft Or, \fqa a solitary hut\f* little house. So they received the¦86909 proper reward for their impiety.\f + \fr 8:33 \ft The Greek text here is perhaps corrupt.\f*
\p
\v 34 The¦86912 thrice-accursed Nikanor¦86915, who'd¦86916 brought the¦86917 thousand merchants to¦86920 buy the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* as slaves,
\v 35 being through the¦86934 help¦86937 of the master humbled by¦86927 them who¦86928 in his eyes were held to be¦86933 of¦86935 least¦86932 account, took off his glorious clothing¦86941, and passing through¦86942 the country, shunning\f + \fr 8:35 \ft Grk. \fqa having made himself solitary.\f* all company like a fugitive slave, arrived at Antiokeia, having, as he thought, had the greatest possible good fortune, though his army was destroyed.\f + \fr 8:35 \ft Or, \fqa having won the greatest possible favour by reason of the destruction of his army\f*
\v 36 He who'd taken on¦86969 himself to make tribute sure for the¦86961 Romaios by the¦86962 captivity of the¦86967 men of Yerushalem published abroad that the¦86968 Yehudim had¦86975 One who fought for them, and¦86980 that because¦86981\f + \fr 8:36 \ft Or, \fqa because of this \ft their \fqa way of life \ft Grk. \fqa because of this manner.\f* this was¦86986 so, the¦86987 Yehudim were invulnerable, because¦86989 they followed¦86991 the¦86992 laws ordained by¦86993 him.
\c 9
\s1 Antiokos' suffering and death
\p
\v 1 Now about¦86997 that¦87001 time¦87000, Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* retreated in\f + \fr 9:1 \ft Or, \fqa with dishonour\f* disorder from¦87006 the region of Paras \add !(Persia)\add*.
\v 2 Because¦87013 he'd entered into the¦87015 city called Persepolis, and he attempted to rob a\f + \fr 9:2 \ft Or, \fqa temples\f* temple and to control the¦87022 city¦87023. Therefore the¦87027 multitudes rushed in and the¦87031 people of the¦87032 country turned to defend themselves with weapons¦87033; and¦87036 it happened¦87037 that¦87039 Antiokos was put to flight by¦87041 the¦87042 people of the¦87045 country and broke his camp with disgrace.
\v 3 While he¦87050 was at Ahmeta, news was brought to him about¦87059 what had happened to Nikanor and¦87057 the¦87058 forces of Timotheos.
\v 4 Being overcome by his anger, he planned to make the Yehudim suffer for the evil¦87072 activities of those who'd¦87074 put him to¦87078 flight. Therefore, with judgement¦87090 from¦87087 heaven¦87088 even now accompanying him, he ordered his charioteer to drive without ceasing until he completed the¦87084 journey¦87085; for he arrogantly said¦87096 this: “I'll make Yerushalem a¦87097 common graveyard of Yehudim when I come¦87101 there¦87102.”
\p
\v 5 But the All-seeing master, the¦87107 god¦87108 of¦87109 Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*, struck¦87111 him with a fatal\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Grk. \fqa remediless.\f* and¦87114 invisible¦87115 stroke. As soon as he'd¦87119 finished speaking this¦87121 word¦87122, an incurable pain of the¦87126 bowels seized him, with bitter torments of the¦87131 inner parts—
\v 6 and¦87138 that¦87136 most justly, for he'd tormented other¦87141 men's bowels with many and strange sufferings.
\v 7 But¦87145 he in no way ceased from his rude insolence. No, he was filled with even more arrogance, breathing fire¦87156 in his passion against¦87160 the¦87161 Yehudim, and¦87163 giving orders to hasten the¦87166 journey¦87167. But¦87169 it happened¦87168 moreover that he¦87172 fell from¦87173 his chariot¦87175 as it rushed along, and¦87178 having a grievous fall was tortured in all¦87182 of the¦87183 members¦87184 of¦87185 his body¦87186.
\v 8 He who'd¦87188 just supposed himself to have the¦87192 waves of the¦87193 sea¦87194 at his bidding because¦87197 he was so superhumanly arrogant, and who thought¦87191 to¦87196 weigh the heights of the mountains¦87206 in a balance, was now¦87190 brought to¦87210 the ground¦87211 and carried in a litter, showing\f + \fr 9:8 \ft Or, \fqa showing manifestly to all the power of God\f* to all¦87219 that the¦87220 power¦87221 was obviously God's¦87218,
\v 9 so¦87223 that worms swarmed out¦87225 of¦87226 the¦87226 impious man's body¦87227, and¦87232 while he was still living¦87233 in¦87234 anguish¦87235 and¦87236 pains, his flesh¦87239 fell off, and¦87243 because of the¦87244 stench all¦87247 the¦87248 army¦87249 turned with loathing from his decay.
\v 10 The man who a little¦87255 before¦87256 supposed himself to touch the¦87257 stars¦87259 of heaven, no one could¦87264 endure to carry¦87270 because¦87265 of¦87265 his intolerable stench.
\v 11 Therefore¦87272 he began in great part to cease from his arrogance, being broken in spirit, and¦87280 to¦87283 come¦87283 to¦87286 knowledge¦87282 under the¦87289 scourge of God, his pains increasing every moment.
\v 12 When¦87291 he himself couldn't¦87296 stand his own smell, he said¦87299 these words: “It's right to¦87301 be subject¦87301 to God¦87303, and¦87304 that one who's mortal¦87306 shouldn't¦87305 think they're¦87307 equal to¦87309 God.”
\p
\v 13 The¦87313 vile man vowed to¦87315 the¦87316 sovereign master¦87320, who now no more¦87317 would have pity on him, saying
\v 14 that the¦87323 holy¦87325 city¦87326, to which¦87327 he was going in haste to lay it even with the ground and to¦87331 make¦87331\f + \fr 9:14 \ft Grk. \fqa build.\f* it a¦87333 common graveyard, he'd declare free.
\v 15 About the¦87337 Yehudim, whom he'd decided not even to count worthy of burial, but to throw them out to the animals with¦87347 their infants for the¦87348 birds to devour, he'd make them all¦87352 equal¦87354 to citizens of Athainai.
\v 16 The holy¦87361 sanctuary, which¦87357 before¦87359 he'd plundered, he'd adorn with best offerings, and¦87366 would restore all¦87371 the¦87367 sacred¦87368 vessels¦87369 many times multiplied, and¦87374 out¦87380 of his own¦87382 revenues would defray the charges that were required for the sacrifices¦87378.
\v 17 Beside all¦87392 this, he said that he'd become a Yehudi \add !(Jew)\add* and would visit every inhabited place¦87393, proclaiming God's¦87399 power¦87400.
\v 18 But when his sufferings did in no¦87401 way cease, for¦87407 God's¦87413 judgement¦87414 had come on him in righteousness, having given up all hope for himself, he¦87417 wrote¦87419 to¦87420 the Yehudim the letter written below, having the nature of a supplication¦87426, to¦87428 this effect:
\p
\v 19 “To the worthy Jewish citizens, Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, king¦87444 and general, wishes much¦87437 joy and¦87439 health¦87440 and prosperity.
\v 20 May you and¦87450 your children¦87452 fare well, and¦87453 may your affairs be¦87458 as you¦87459 wish¦87460. Having my hope¦87470 in heaven¦87468,
\v 21 I¦87472 remembered¦87483 with affection your honour¦87478 and¦87479 good will. Returning out¦87486 of the region of Paras \add !(Persia)\add*, and¦87492 being taken with an annoying sickness, I deemed it necessary¦87497 to¦87498 take thought for the¦87500 common¦87501 safety of all¦87502,
\v 22 not despairing of myself¦87508, but¦87509 having¦87510 great hope¦87512 to escape from the¦87514 sickness.
\v 23 But¦87517 considering that¦87518 my father¦87521 also¦87519, at the¦87520 time¦87524 he led an army into the¦87526 upper country, appointed¦87530 his successor,
\v 24 to the end that¦87533, if¦87534 anything fell out contrary to expectation, or¦87538 if any unwelcome tidings were brought, the¦87544 people in the¦87546 country¦87547, knowing to whom the¦87550 state had been left, might not¦87552 be troubled,
\v 25 and¦87555, moreover, observing how the princes who are along the borders and neighbours to my kingdom¦87564 watch for opportunities and look for the future event, I've appointed my son¦87573 Antiokos to be king¦87576, whom I often¦87578 entrusted and¦87588 commended¦87589 to¦87590 most of you when¦87591 I was hurrying to¦87592 the¦87594 upper provinces. I've written to him what's written below.
\v 26 I therefore¦87597 urge you¦87598 and¦87599 beg you, having in your remembrance the¦87602 benefits done to you in common and¦87605 severally, to preserve your present good will, each¦87607 of you, towards¦87612 me and¦87614 my son¦87616.
\v 27 Because I'm persuaded¦87618 that¦87625 he¦87620 in gentleness and¦87622 kindness will follow my¦87626 purpose and treat you¦87629 with moderation and kindness.
\p
\v 28 So the¦87630 murderer¦87633 and¦87634 blasphemer, having endured the¦87636 most intense sufferings¦87638, even as he had dealt with other¦87640 men, ended his life¦87651 among the¦87645 mountains¦87646 by a most piteous fate in a strange land.
\v 29 Filippos¦87656 \add !(Philip)\add* his foster brother took¦87658 the¦87654 body¦87655 home and¦87661 then, fearing the¦87663 son¦87664 of Antiokos, he withdrew himself to¦87666 Ptolemaios \add !(Ptolemy)\add* Filometor in Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add*.
\c 10
\s1 The temple is cleansed and rededicated
\p
\v 1 Then¦87674 Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* and¦87675 those who¦87676 were with¦87677 him, the master leading them on¦87684, recovered the temple¦87685 and¦87687 the¦87688 city¦87689.
\v 2 They pulled down the¦87690 altars that'd been built in the¦87693 marketplace¦87694 by¦87696 the¦87697 foreigners, and¦87701 also¦87701 the sacred enclosures.
\v 3 Having cleansed the¦87705 sanctuary, they made¦87710 another altar¦87709 of sacrifice¦87720. Striking\f + \fr 10:3 \ft Grk. \fqa firing.\f* flint and starting a fire¦87715, they offered sacrifices after¦87721 they'd ceased for two years, burnt incense¦87725, lit lamps, and set out¦87716 the¦87729 show bread¦87730.
\v 4 When¦87749 they'd done¦87736 these things, they fell prostrate and implored the¦87738 master that they might fall no more into such¦87745 evils; but¦87747 that, if¦87748 they ever did sin, they might be chastened by¦87752 him with¦87754 forbearance, and¦87757 not¦87758 be delivered¦87763 to blaspheming and barbarous heathen.
\v 5 Now on¦87764 the same day¦87767 that the¦87768 sanctuary was profaned by¦87770 foreigners, on that very day it happened¦87773 that the¦87781 sanctuary was cleansed¦87779, even on the¦87783 twenty-fifth day of¦87787 the¦87787 same month, which¦87790 is¦87791 Kislev.
\v 6 They observed eight¦87798 days¦87797 with¦87794 gladness¦87795 in the manner of the feast of tabernacles, remembering¦87801 how¦87802 not\f + \fr 10:6 \ft Or, \fqa not long before they kept the feast of tabernacles by wandering\f* long before¦87803, during the feast of tabernacles, they were wandering in¦87810 the¦87811 mountains¦87812 and¦87813 in¦87814 the¦87815 caves¦87816 like wild¦87817 animals¦87817.
\v 7 Therefore carrying wands wreathed with leaves, and¦87823 beautiful branches¦87824, and¦87827 palm fronds also, they offered up hymns of thanksgiving to¦87829 him who'd¦87832 successfully brought to pass the¦87835 cleansing of his own place¦87837.
\v 8 They ordained also¦87839 with¦87840 a public statute and¦87843 decree, for all¦87845 the¦87846 nation¦87849 of the¦87854 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, that they should observe these days¦87855 every year¦87851.
\p
\v 9 Such were the events of the end of Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add*, who was called Epifanes \add !(Epiphanes)\add*.
\s1 Fighting under the new King Antiokos
\p
\v 10 Now¦87867 we'll declare what happened under Antiokos Eupator,\f + \fr 10:10 \ft That is, \fqa son of a good father.\f* who¦87871 proved himself a son¦87874 of¦87876 that¦87882 ungodly man, and will summarise the¦87883 main evils¦87885 of the wars¦87884.
\v 11 Because this man, when he¦87886 succeeded to¦87888 the kingdom¦87889, appointed¦87890 one¦87895 Lusias to be chancellor and¦87897 supreme governor of Coele-Syria and¦87899 Foinikai.
\v 12 Because Ptolemaios¦87903 \add !(Ptolemy)\add* who¦87905 was called¦87906 Makron¦87907, setting an example of observing justice towards¦87912 the¦87913 Yehudim because¦87915 of¦87915 the¦87916 wrong¦87920 that'd¦87923 been done to them, endeavoured to deal with them on peaceful terms.
\v 13 Whereupon being accused¦87929 by¦87930 the¦87931 king's friends¦87932\f + \fr 10:13 \ft See 2 Maccabees 8:9\f* before Eupator, and¦87936 hearing¦87939 himself called traitor¦87937 at every turn because¦87940 he'd abandoned Kupros \add !(Cyprus)\add* which Filometor had entrusted to him, and had withdrawn himself to Antiokos Epifanes, and failing to uphold the honour of¦87946 his office,\f + \fr 10:13 \ft The Greek text here is corrupt.\f* he took poison and¦87949 did away with¦87950 himself¦87963.
\p
\v 14 But when Gorgias¦87967 was¦87969 made governor of the¦87971 district, he maintained a force of mercenaries, and¦87974 at every turn kept up war with¦87976 the¦87977 Yehudim.
\v 15 Together¦87980 with him the¦87984 Edomim also, being masters of important strongholds¦87988, harassed the¦87991 Yehudim; and¦87993 received those who'd¦87994 taken¦87998 refuge from¦87996 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, they endeavoured to¦88000 keep up the war.
\v 16 But Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* and his men, having made¦88006 solemn supplication and¦88008 having implored God¦88011 to¦88014 fight on their side, rushed on¦88015 the¦88016 strongholds of the¦88017 Edomim.
\v 17 Assaulting them vigorously, they took control of the positions, and kept off all¦88029 who¦88031 fought on¦88032 the¦88033 wall, and¦88038 killed those whom¦88039 they encountered, killing no¦88043 fewer than twenty thousand.
\p
\v 18 Because no¦88049 fewer than nine thousand had fled into two¦88054 very strong towers having¦88064 everything needed for a siege,
\v 19 Makabi, having left¦88088 Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add* and Yosef \add !(Joseph)\add*, and also Zakay and those who¦88079 were with¦88080 him, a force sufficient¦88082 to¦88083 besiege them, departed himself to places¦88069 where he was most needed.
\v 20 But Shim'on and those who¦88092 were with him, yielding to covetousness, were bribed by¦88095 some of those who¦88097 were in¦88098 the¦88099 towers, and¦88104 receiving seventy thousand drachmas¦88106, let¦88108 some of them slip away.
\v 21 But¦88112 when word was brought to Makabi of¦88121 what was done, he gathered the¦88121 people's¦88122 leaders together, and accused those men of having sold their kindred for money¦88125 by setting their enemies free to¦88131 fight against them.
\v 22 So he¦88134 killed¦88139 these men for having turned traitors, and¦88140 immediately took possession of the¦88142 two¦88143 towers.
\v 23 Prospering with his weapons in¦88151 everything he undertook, he destroyed¦88155 more than twenty thousand in¦88156 the¦88157 two¦88158 strongholds.
\s1 Yehudah defeats Timotheos
\p
\v 24 Now Timotheos¦88163, who'd¦88165 been defeated by¦88168 the¦88169 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* before¦88166, having gathered together foreign forces in great multitudes, and¦88175 having collected the cavalry which¦88185 belonged to Asia, not a few, came as though he'd take Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add* by force of weapons.
\v 25 But¦88191 as he¦88196 drew near, Makabi and his men sprinkled dirt on their heads¦88204 and¦88206 girded their loins¦88208 with sackcloth, in supplication to God¦88200,
\v 26 and falling down on¦88211 the¦88212 step in front of¦88214 the¦88214 altar¦88215, implored him to become gracious\f + \fr 10:26 \ft Grk. \fqa propitious.\f* to them, and be¦88221\f + \fr 10:26 \ft See Exodus 23:22.\f* an enemy to their enemies and¦88226 an adversary to their adversaries, as¦88230 the¦88231 law¦88232 declares.
\v 27 Rising from¦88236 their prayer¦88238 they took¦88239 up¦88239 their weapons¦88241, and advanced some distance from¦88243 the¦88244 city¦88245. When¦88249 they'd come near to their enemies, they\f + \fr 10:27 \ft Grk. \fqa were by themselves.\f* halted.
\v 28 When the dawn was now¦88255 breaking, the¦88257 two armies joined in battle, the¦88262 one¦88263 part having¦88265 this, beside virtue, for a pledge of success and¦88267 victory, that¦88271 they'd fled to¦88272 the master for refuge, the others making their passion their leader in the fight.
\p
\v 29 When¦88285 the¦88289 battle became strong, there appeared¦88288 out¦88291 of heaven¦88292 to¦88293 their adversaries five¦88297 splendid men¦88296 on horses¦88294 with bridles of gold, and¦88299\f + \fr 10:29 \ft Some authorities read \fqa and leading on the Jews; who also, taking.\f* two¦88304 of them, leading on the Yehudim,
\v 30 and¦88305 taking¦88309 Makabi in the¦88306 middle¦88308 of them, and covering him with their own armour, guarded him from wounds, while they shot arrows and thunderbolts at the enemies. Because this reason, they were blinded and¦88322 thrown into¦88327 confusion, and were cut to¦88330 pieces, filled with bewilderment.
\v 31 Twenty thousand and¦88332 five¦88336 hundred¦88336 were slain, beside six¦88339 hundred¦88339 cavalry.
\p
\v 32 Timotheos¦88343 himself fled into a stronghold¦88348 called Gezer, a fortress of great strength, where\f + \fr 10:32 \ft See ver. 37.\f* Kaireas was in command¦88347.
\v 33 Then Makabi and his men were glad and laid siege to the fortress for four¦88365 days¦88364.
\v 34 Those who were within¦88368, trusting in the¦88369 strength of¦88371 the¦88371 place¦88372, blasphemed¦88375 greatly, and¦88376 hurled out impious words¦88377.
\v 35 But¦88381 at dawn of the¦88382 fifth day¦88384, certain young men of Makabi’ company, inflamed with anger because¦88394 of¦88394 the¦88395 blasphemies¦88396, assaulted the¦88398 wall with masculine force and with furious\f + \fr 10:35 \ft Grk. \fqa passion as of wild animals.\f* anger, and cut¦88406 down whoever came in their way.
\v 36 Others¦88407 climbing up in¦88411 the same way, while the enemies were distracted with¦88414 those who'd¦88415 made their way within¦88416, set fire to the¦88418 towers, and¦88420 kindled fires that¦88424 burnt¦88426 the¦88427 blasphemers alive¦88423, while others broke open the¦88429 gates¦88430, and¦88433, having given entrance to the rest of the band, occupied the¦88438 city¦88439.
\v 37 They killed Timotheos, who¦88441 was hidden in¦88444 a cistern, and¦88448 his brother¦88451 Kaireas, and¦88453 Apolofanes.
\v 38 When¦88457 they'd accomplished these things, they blessed the master with¦88459 hymns and¦88461 thanksgiving, blessing him who provides great¦88467 benefits to¦88475 Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* and¦88471 gives¦88475 them the¦88472 victory¦88473.
\c 11
\s1 Lusias is defeated and seeks peace
\p
\v 1 Now after¦88476 a very little¦88477 time¦88480, Lusias¦88481, the¦88483 king's¦88484 guardian, kinsman, and chancellor, being very displeased about¦88483 the¦88489 things¦88490 that'd¦88495 happened,
\v 2 collected about¦88498 eighty thousand infantry and¦88502 all¦88505 his cavalry and came¦88506 against¦88507 the¦88508 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, planing to¦88510 make the¦88511 city¦88513 a home for Hellains \add !(Greeks)\add*,
\v 3 and to levy tribute on the temple¦88519, as¦88521 on\f + \fr 11:3 \ft Or, \fqa on all the sacred places of the heathen\f* the¦88522 other sacred places of the¦88524 nations¦88525, and¦88528 to put up the high priesthood for sale every year¦88532.
\v 4 He took no¦88534 account of¦88537 God's¦88538 power¦88539, but¦88541 was puffed up with his ten thousands of infantry, his thousands of cavalry, and¦88551 his eighty¦88555 elephants.
\v 5 Coming into¦88556 Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add* and approaching Beyt-Tsur, which was¦88565 a strong place and¦88570 about¦88573 five¦88575 stadia\f + \fr 11:5 \ft One stadia was roughly 189 metres or 618 feet, so 5 stadia was about a little less than 1 km or a little more than half a mile.\f* away¦88569 from¦88569 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, he pressed it hard.
\p
\v 6 When Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* and¦88579 his men learnt that he¦88586 was besieging the strongholds, they and all the people with lamentations and tears made supplication to the master to¦88601 send¦88601 a good angel¦88600 to¦88602 save Yisrael.
\v 7 Makabi himself took¦88611 up¦88611 weapons¦88613 first¦88608, and exhorted the others to put themselves in jeopardy together with him and¦88625 help their kindred; and¦88626 they went out with him very willingly.
\v 8 As they were¦88635 there, close to¦88636 Yerushalem, a horseman appeared¦88636 at their head in¦88640 white¦88641 clothing¦88642, brandishing\f + \fr 11:8 \ft Grk. \fqa a panoply.\f* weapons of gold¦88644.
\v 9 They all¦88648 together¦88646 praised the¦88650 merciful God¦88652, and¦88653 were yet more strengthened in heart, being ready¦88670 to assail\f + \fr 11:9 \ft Grk. \fqa wound.\f* not only¦88658 men but¦88660 the¦88663 wildest animals and¦88665 walls¦88667 of iron¦88666,
\v 10 they advanced in¦88672 array, having him who's¦88674 in heaven¦88676 to¦88678 fight on their side, for the¦88681 master had mercy on them.
\v 11 Hurling themselves like¦88683 lions¦88683 against the enemy, they killed eleven thousand infantry and one thousand and six hundred cavalry, and forced all¦88703 the rest to¦88705 flee¦88705.
\v 12 Most of them escaped wounded and¦88708 naked¦88711. Lusias¦88717 himself also escaped by shameful flight.
\v 13 But¦88723 as he was¦88724 a man not void of understanding, pondering the¦88728 defeat which had befallen him, and¦88733 considering that¦88737 the¦88739 Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add* couldn't¦88741 be overcome because the Almighty God¦88742 fought on their side, he¦88744 sent again
\v 14 and persuaded them to come to terms on condition that all¦88749 their rights were acknowledged, and¦88752 promised that he'd also¦88754 persuade the¦88755 king¦88756 to become their friend¦88758.\f + \fr 11:14 \ft The Greek text here is corrupt.\f*
\v 15 Makabi gave consent on¦88766 all¦88767 the conditions which¦88768 Lusias¦88770 proposed to¦88771 him, being careful of¦88772 the¦88772 common good; for¦88776 whatever requests Makabi delivered in writing to Lusias about¦88784 the¦88785 Yehudim the¦88788 king¦88789 allowed.
\s1 Letters confirming the peace agreement
\p
\v 16 The¦88792 letter written to the¦88794 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* from¦88797 Lusias was¦88790 to this effect:
\p “Lusias¦88804 to the¦88805 people¦88806\f + \fr 11:16 \ft Grk. \fqa multitude.\f* of the¦88807 Yehudim, greetings.
\v 17 Yohan \add !(John)\add* and¦88811 Avshalom \add !(Absalom)\add*, who¦88813 were sent¦88814 from¦88815 you¦88816, having delivered¦88817 the¦88818 document written below, made request about¦88822 the¦88823 things written there¦88825.
\v 18 Whatever things therefore¦88829 needed to be brought before the¦88832 king¦88833 I declared to him, and what things were¦88838 possible he allowed.
\v 19 If¦88841 then¦88843 you'll all preserve your good will towards¦88846 the¦88847 government, I'll also¦88850 endeavour in the¦88852 future to contribute to your good.
\v 20 About this, I've given order in detail, both to these men and to those who¦88868 are sent from¦88869 me, to confer with¦88869 you¦88872.
\v 21 Farewell¦88873. Written in the one hundred and forty-eighth year, on the twenty-fourth day of the month Dioskorinthios.”\f + \fr 11:21 \ft This month name is not found elsewhere, and is perhaps corrupt.\f*
\p
\v 22 And¦88882 the¦88883 king's¦88884 letter contained these words:
\p “King¦88888 Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* to his brother¦88891 Lusias, greetings.
\v 23 Seeing that our father¦88895 passed to¦88900 the gods¦88898 having the¦88901 wish that the¦88903 subjects of his kingdom¦88904 should\f + \fr 11:23 \ft Or, \fqa should not be disquieted but\f* be undisturbed and give themselves to the care of their own¦88911 affairs,
\v 24 we, having heard that the¦88914 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* don't¦88916 consent to our father's¦88920 purpose to turn them to the¦88922 customs of the Hellains \add !(Greeks)\add*, but¦88925 choose rather their own way of living, and¦88930 make request that the¦88936 customs of their law be allowed to them—
\v 25 choosing therefore¦88940 that¦88942 this¦88943 nation¦88944 also should be¦88947 free from disturbance, we determine that their temple¦88951 is to be restored to them, and¦88954 that they live according¦88956 to¦88956 the¦88957 customs¦88962 that were in the¦88959 days of their ancestors¦88960.
\v 26 You'll therefore¦88964 do well¦88963 to¦88965 send messengers to¦88967 them and¦88969 give¦88970 them the right¦88971 hand of friendship, that¦88972 they, knowing our¦88975 mind, may be¦88979 of good heart, and¦88980 gladly¦88981 occupy themselves with¦88983 the conduct of their own¦88986 affairs.”
\p
\v 27 And to the¦88990 nation¦88991, the¦88993 king's¦88994 letter was¦88997 as¦88996 follows:
\p “King¦88998 Antiokos to the¦89000 senate of¦89001 the¦89002 Yehudim and¦89004 to the other Yehudim, greetings.
\v 28 If¦89009 you're¦89011 all well, it's¦89016 as we desire. We ourselves also¦89017 are in good health.
\v 29 Menelaos¦89022 informed us that your desire was¦89026 to¦89027 return home and follow your own¦89029 business.
\v 30 They therefore¦89031 who depart home up to the thirtieth day of Xanthikos will have our friendship,\f + \fr 11:30 \ft Grk. \fqa right hand.\f* with¦89038 full permission
\v 31 that the¦89044 Yehudim use their own foods and¦89047 observe their own laws, even as¦89049 formerly. None of them will be in any way¦89058 molested for the¦89061 things that have been done in ignorance.
\v 32 Moreover I've sent Menelaos also, that¦89066 he may encourage you¦89069.
\v 33 Farewell¦89070. Written in the one hundred and¦89077 forty-eighth year, on the fifteenth day of Xanthikos.”
\p
\v 34 The¦89082 Romaios also sent to them a letter in these words:
\p “Kointos¦89089 Memios and Titos¦89091 Manios, ambassadors of the Romaios, to the¦89095 people¦89096 of the¦89097 Yehudim, greetings.
\v 35 In regard to the things which¦89101 Lusias¦89102 the king's¦89106 kinsman granted you¦89108, we¦89110 also¦89109 give consent.
\v 36 But¦89113 as for the things which he judged¦89114 should be referred to¦89114 the¦89116 king¦89117, send someone¦89119 promptly, after you've¦89128 considered them, that we¦89129 may publish such decrees as are appropriate for your case; for we're on our way to¦89132 Antiokeia.
\v 37 Therefore send someone¦89138 with speed, that¦89139 we¦89141 also may learn what's¦89144 your mind.
\v 38 Farewell.\f + \fr 11:38 \ft Grk. \fqa Be in good health.\f* Written in the one hundred and¦89153 forty-eighth year, on the fifteenth day of Xanthikos.
\c 12
\s1 Yehudah punishes attacks on the Yehudim
\p
\v 1 So when this agreement had been¦89155 made, Lusias¦89161 departed to¦89163 the¦89164 king¦89165, and¦89167 the Yehudim went about¦89169 their farming.
\p
\v 2 But some of the governors of districts, Timotheos¦89178 and¦89179 Apolonios the¦89181 son of¦89182 Gennaios, and¦89185 also¦89185 Hieronumos¦89186 and¦89187 Demofon, and¦89190 beside them Nikanor¦89192 Kupros' \add !(Cyprus)\add* governor, wouldn't allow¦89196 them to enjoy tranquillity and live in peace.
\v 3 Men of Yafo \add !(Joppa)\add* perpetrated this great impiety: they invited the Yehudim who lived among them to go with¦89223 their wives and¦89225 children¦89226 into the boats which¦89227 they'd provided, as though they had no ill will towards¦89217 them.
\v 4 When¦89234\f + \fr 12:4 \ft Grk. \fqa they also.\f* the Yehudim,\f + \fr 12:4 \ft Grk. \fqa after.\f* relying on the public vote of the¦89237 city¦89238, accepted the invitation, as men desiring¦89246 to¦89246 live in peace and¦89247 suspecting nothing¦89248, they took them out to sea and drowned not less than two¦89258 hundred¦89258 of them.
\v 5 When¦89260 Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* heard of the cruelty done to his fellow-countrymen, giving command to the¦89269 men¦89272 that were with him
\v 6 and¦89273 calling¦89274 on¦89274 God¦89278 the¦89275 righteous Judge¦89277, he came¦89279 against¦89280 the¦89281 murderers of his kindred, and¦89285 set the¦89286 harbour¦89288 on¦89287 fire at night, burnt¦89290 the boats, and¦89291 put to the¦89292 sword those who'd¦89295 fled there¦89297.
\v 7 But¦89301 when the town gates were closed, he withdrew, intending to come again¦89306 to root out the¦89309 whole community of the¦89311 men of Yafo.
\v 8 But¦89316 learning that¦89318 the¦89321 men of Yavneh intended to¦89323 do the same thing to¦89324 the Yehudim who lived among¦89319 them,
\v 9 he attacked the¦89330 Jamnites at night¦89332, and set¦89333 fire to¦89333 the¦89335 harbour¦89336 together with¦89337 the¦89338 fleet, so¦89340 that the¦89342 glare of¦89344 the light was¦89350 seen at Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, two¦89351 hundred¦89351 and forty¦89352 furlongs\f + \fr 12:9 \ft a furlong is about 201 metres or 220 yards, so 240 furlongs is about 48 km. or 30 miles\f* distant.
\s1 Victories in the east
\p
\v 10 Now when¦89354 they'd drawn off nine¦89357 furlongs\f + \fr 12:10 \ft a furlong is about 201 metres or 220 yards, so 9 furlongs is about 1.8 km. or 1 1/8 miles\f* from¦89353 there¦89353, as they marched against¦89361 Timotheos, an army of Aravim attacked him, no¦89367 fewer than five¦89370 thousand¦89370 infantry and¦89372 five¦89373 hundred¦89373 cavalry.
\v 11 And when a hard battle had been¦89374 fought, and¦89375 Yehudah and¦89378 his company, by the help of God¦89387, had good success, the¦89391 nomads being overcome implored Yehudah to grant them friendship, promising to¦89395 give¦89395 him livestock, and to help his\f + \fr 12:11 \ft Grk. \fqa them.\f* people in¦89405 all other ways.
\v 12 So¦89411 Yehudah, thinking¦89412 that¦89413 they'd indeed be profitable in many things, agreed to live in peace¦89420 with¦89422 them; and¦89424 receiving pledges of friendship they departed to¦89425 their tents¦89429.
\p
\v 13 He also attacked a certain city¦89437, strong and fenced with earthworks and walls, and inhabited by a mixed multitude of various nations¦89445. It was named¦89447 Kaspin¦89449.
\v 14 Those who were within¦89452, trusting in the strength of the walls and their store of provisions, behaved themselves rudely towards Yehudah and those who were with him, railing, and¦89470 furthermore blaspheming¦89472 and¦89473 speaking¦89474 impious words.
\v 15 But Yehudah and his company, calling on the¦89484 great¦89485 Sovereign of¦89486 the¦89486 world¦89487, who¦89489 without¦89490 rams and¦89492 cunning engines of war hurled down Yeriho \add !(Jericho)\add* in the times¦89501 of Yeshua \add !(Jesus)\add*, rushed wildly against the¦89504 wall.
\v 16 Having taken the¦89508 city¦89509 by God's¦89512 will, they made unspeakable slaughter, so¦89517 much that the¦89518 adjoining lake¦89520, which was two¦89525 furlongs\f + \fr 12:16 \ft a furlong is about 201 metres or 220 yards, so 2 furlongs is about 402 metres or 1/4 mile\f* broad, appeared to be filled with the deluge of blood¦89527.
\s1 Another victory over Timotheos
\p
\v 17 When they'd gone seven hundred and¦89531 fifty¦89535 furlongs\f + \fr 12:17 \ft a furlong is about 201 metres or 220 yards, so 750 furlongs is about 151 km. or 94 miles\f* from¦89530 there¦89530, they made their way to Karax, to the¦89541 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* that are called Tubieni.\f + \fr 12:17 \ft That is, \fqa men of Tob: \ft see Judges 11:3, 2 Samuel 10:6, and compare 1 Maccabees 5:13.\f*
\v 18 They didn't find Timotheos in that¦89549 district, for he had by then¦89554 departed from¦89555 the¦89556 district without accomplishing anything, but¦89560 had left behind a very strong garrison in¦89562 one¦89563 place¦89564.
\v 19 But Dositheos¦89568 and Sosipatros¦89571, who were captains under Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, went out and destroyed those who'd been left by¦89580 Timotheos in¦89583 the stronghold, more than ten thousand men¦89589.
\v 20 Makabi, arranging his own army in divisions, set these\f + \fr 12:20 \ft Grk. \fqa them.\f* two over the¦89590 bands, and¦89591 marched in¦89597 haste against¦89600 Timotheos, who¦89601 had¦89608 with¦89617 him one hundred and twenty thousand infantry and two thousand and five¦89619 hundred¦89619 cavalry.
\v 21 When¦89621 Timotheos¦89626 heard of the approach of Yehudah, he at once sent away the women¦89629 and¦89630 the¦89631 children¦89632 with the¦89634 baggage into the¦89638 fortress called Karnayim;\f + \fr 12:21 \ft Compare \fqa Carnain, \ft 1 Maccabees 5:26, 43, 44.\f* for¦89642 the¦89646 place¦89647 was hard to besiege and difficult of access because¦89648 of¦89648 the¦89649 narrowness of the¦89650 approaches on all¦89651 sides.
\v 22 When the band of Yehudah, who led the first¦89660 division, appeared¦89674 in sight, and when terror and fear¦89663 came on¦89664 the¦89665 enemy, because the¦89670 manifestation of¦89671 him who sees all¦89672 things came on¦89676 them, they fled in every direction, carried this way and that, so¦89684 that they were often¦89685 injured by¦89686 their own¦89688 men, and¦89690 pierced with the points of their own swords.
\v 23 Yehudah continued the pursuit more vigorously, putting the wicked wretches to the sword, and¦89706 he destroyed as many as thirty thousand men¦89710.
\p
\v 24 Timotheos¦89714 himself, falling in with the company of Dositheos and¦89720 Sosipatros, implored them with¦89723 much¦89724 crafty guile to let him go with his life, because¦89729 he had in his power the¦89730 parents¦89733 of many of them and the kindred of some. “Otherwise, he said, little regard will be shown\f + \fr 12:24 \ft Or, \fqa have been shown\f* to these.”\f + \fr 12:24 \ft Grk. \fqa and the result will be that these be disregarded. \ft The Greek text here is perhaps corrupt.\f*
\v 25 So when he¦89746 had with many words confirmed the¦89749 agreement to¦89754 restore them without harm, they let him go that they might save their kindred.
\p
\v 26 Then¦89762 Yehudah, marching against¦89763 Karnayim\f + \fr 12:26 \ft Compare \fqa Carnain, \ft 1 Maccabees 5:26, 43, 44.\f* and¦89766 Atergatis' temple, killed twenty-five thousand¦89774 people.
\v 27 After¦89776 he'd¦89778 put these to flight and¦89780 destroyed¦89781 them, he marched against¦89785 Efron¦89786 also, a strong city¦89787, wherein\f + \fr 12:27 \ft The Greek text here is perhaps corrupt.\f* were multitudes¦89795 of people of all nations. Stalwart young men placed¦89805 on¦89789\f + \fr 12:27 \ft Grk. \fqa in front of.\f* the¦89799 walls made a vigorous defence. There were great stores of war engines and¦89807 darts there.
\v 28 But¦89813 calling on the¦89814 Sovereign who¦89816 with¦89817 might shatters the¦89820 strength¦89818\f + \fr 12:28 \ft Some authorities read \fqa weight.\f* of the¦89821\f + \fr 12:28 \ft Or, \fqa his enemies\f* enemy, they took¦89824 the¦89825 city¦89826 into their hands, and killed as many as twenty-five thousand¦89836 of those who were in it.
\p
\v 29 Setting out from¦89839 there¦89839, they marched in haste against¦89841 Beyt-Shean, which is six hundred furlongs\f + \fr 12:29 \ft a furlong is about 201 metres or 220 yards, so 600 furlongs is about 121 km. or 75 miles\f* away¦89845 from¦89845 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 30 But¦89850 when the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* who were settled¦89853 there¦89852 testified of the good will that¦89855 the¦89856 Scythopolitans had¦89858 shown towards them, and¦89862 of their kind treatment of them in¦89863 the times¦89867 of their misfortune,
\v 31 they gave thanks, and further exhorted them to remain¦89878 well disposed towards the race for the future. Then they went¦89884 up to Yerushalem, the feast of weeks being¦89891 close at hand.
\s1 Yehudah fights Gorgias
\p
\v 32 But after¦89893 the¦89895 feast called Pentecost, they marched in haste against¦89899 Gorgias Edom's governor.
\v 33 He came out¦89905 with¦89907 three thousand infantry and¦89911 four¦89912 hundred¦89912 cavalry.
\v 34 When¦89913 they'd set themselves in array, it happened¦89915 that a few of the¦89918 Yehudim fell.
\v 35 A certain Dositheos¦89920, one¦89922 of Bacenor's company,\f + \fr 12:35 \ft The Greek text is uncertain.\f* who was on horseback and was a strong man¦89927, pressed hard on Gorgias, and¦89928 taking hold of¦89931 his cloak dragged him along by main force. While he¦89938 planned¦89941 to¦89944 take¦89944 the¦89946 accursed man alive, one¦89949 of the Thracian cavalry bore down on him and disabled his shoulder, and so Gorgias¦89958 escaped to Mareshah.\f + \fr 12:35 \ft Compare 1 Maccabees 5:65.\f*
\p
\v 36 When¦89962 those who¦89964 were with Ezra had been fighting long and¦89968 were¦89970 weary, Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* called¦89971 on the master to show himself, fighting on¦89971 their side and¦89978 leading in the¦89980 battle.
\v 37 Then in the¦89983 language of his ancestors he raised the¦89986 battle cry joined with¦89987 hymns. Then he rushed against Gorgias’ troops when they weren't expecting it, and¦89991 put them to flight.
\s1 Prayers and an offering for the dead
\p
\v 38 Yehudah gathered his army and¦90002 came to¦90003 the¦90004 city¦90009 of Adulam.\f + \fr 12:38 \ft Grk. \fqa Odollam.\f* As the¦90010 seventh day was coming on, they purified themselves according¦90014 to¦90014 the¦90015 custom, and kept the¦90019 Sabbath¦90020 there.
\p
\v 39 On the¦90022 following day, when¦90023\f + \fr 12:39 \ft The Greek text here is uncertain.\f* it'd¦90024 become necessary, Yehudah and his company came¦90025 to take up the bodies¦90040 of those who'd fallen, and¦90042\f + \fr 12:39 \ft Or, \fqa and to bring them back to be with their kinsmen in the sepulchres\f* in company with¦90043 their kinsmen to bring them back to the sepulchres of their ancestors.
\v 40 But¦90052 under¦90056 the¦90057 clothes of each¦90053 one of the dead they found¦90051 consecrated\f + \fr 12:40 \ft Perhaps these were consecrated images of the idols.\f* tokens of the idols¦90063 of Yavneh, which¦90065 the¦90066 law¦90067 forbids the Yehudim to have anything to do with. It became clear to all¦90073 that it was¦90075 for¦90076 this cause¦90079 that they'd fallen.
\v 41 All¦90082 therefore¦90083, blessing the ways of the master, the righteous Judge, who makes¦90092 manifest¦90091 the things that are hidden¦90090,
\v 42 turned themselves to supplication, praying that the sin committed might be wholly blotted¦90101 out¦90101. The¦90102 noble Yehudah exhorted the¦90107 multitude¦90108 to¦90112 keep themselves from sin, for they'd seen with their own eyes what happened because¦90118 of¦90118 the sin¦90122 of those who'd fallen.
\v 43 When he'd made¦90123 a collection man by man to¦90125 the sum of¦90131 two thousand¦90131 drachmas¦90130 of silver, he sent¦90132 to Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add* to offer a sacrifice¦90138 for sin¦90137, doing very well¦90140 and¦90141 honourably in this, in that he took thought for¦90144 the resurrection¦90145.
\v 44 Because if¦90147 he wasn't expecting that those who'd¦90150 fallen would rise¦90152 again, it'd be¦90156 superfluous and¦90157 idle to¦90161 pray¦90161 for the dead¦90160.
\v 45 But if he was looking forward to an honourable memorial of gratitude laid up for those who¦90164 die\f + \fr 12:45 \ft Grk. \fqa fall asleep.\f* in\f + \fr 12:45 \ft Or, \fqa on the side of godliness\f* godliness, then the¦90174 thought¦90175 was holy and godly. Therefore he made the¦90178 atoning sacrifice for those who'd¦90180 died, that¦90183 they might be released from their sin¦90184.
\c 13
\s1 Antiokos invades and Menelaos is executed
\p
\v 1 In the¦90186 one hundred and forty-ninth year¦90193, news was brought to¦90194 Yehudah and his company that Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* Eupator was coming with¦90203 multitudes against¦90205 Yehudah,
\v 2 and¦90208 with¦90209 him Lusias his guardian and¦90214 chancellor, each¦90218 having¦90219 a Greek force of one hundred and¦90225 ten thousand infantry, five¦90227 thousand¦90227 and¦90229 three hundred cavalry, twenty-two¦90231 elephants, and¦90233 three hundred chariots armed with scythes.
\p
\v 3 And¦90236 Menelaos¦90237 also¦90238 joined himself with them, and¦90241 with¦90243 great hypocrisy encouraged Antiokos, not for the saving of his country, but¦90254 because he thought that he'd be set over the¦90256 government.
\v 4 But¦90260 the¦90262 King¦90263 of¦90267 kings stirred up Antiokos' anger against¦90269 the¦90270 wicked wretch. When¦90272 Lusias informed him that this¦90275 man was¦90277 the cause of all¦90278 the¦90279 evils¦90280, the king commanded to bring him to Beroia, and to put him to death in¦90285 the¦90286 way customary in that place¦90287.
\v 5 Now there's¦90293 in¦90295 that¦90296 place¦90297 a tower¦90298 that's fifty¦90299 cubits¦90300 high, full¦90301 of ashes, and it had¦90306 all¦90308 around it a circular\f + \fr 13:5 \ft Grk. \fqa contrivance or machine.\f* rim sloping steeply on every side¦90308 into the¦90311 ashes.
\v 6 Here¦90313 one who's¦90314 guilty of sacrilege or¦90318 notorious for¦90316 other crimes is pushed down to¦90317 destruction¦90328.
\v 7 By such¦90329 a fate it happened¦90333 that the breaker of the law, Menelaos, died, without obtaining so much as a grave in the¦90336 earth¦90337, and that justly;
\v 8 for¦90343 since¦90342 he'd perpetrated many sins¦90349 against\f + \fr 13:8 \ft Grk. \fqa about.\f* the¦90347 altar¦90348, whose fire¦90352 and¦90355 whose ashes¦90357 were holy, he received his death¦90361 in¦90358 ashes¦90359.
\s1 Yehudah resists the invasion
\p
\v 9 Now the¦90366 king¦90367,\f + \fr 13:9 \ft Some authorities read \fqa indignant.\f* infuriated in spirit, was coming¦90369 with intent to¦90369 inflict on the¦90370 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* the¦90372 very worst of¦90374 the¦90374 sufferings that'd been done in his father's¦90375 time.
\v 10 But¦90382 when Yehudah heard of these things, he¦90384 commanded the¦90386 multitude¦90387 to¦90392 call¦90392 on¦90392 the¦90393 master day¦90389 and¦90397 night¦90391, if ever at any other time, so now¦90398 to help those who were at the point of being deprived of the¦90401 law¦90402, their country, and the holy¦90407 temple¦90406,
\v 11 and¦90410 not to allow the¦90411 people¦90415 who'd¦90418 just begun to¦90422 be¦90422 revived to fall into the hands of those profane heathen.
\v 12 So when they had all¦90423 done the¦90425 same thing together¦90428, begging\f + \fr 13:12 \ft Grk. \fqa and implored.\f* the¦90431 merciful master with¦90434 weeping and¦90436 fastings¦90437 and¦90438 prostration for three¦90442 days¦90441 without ceasing, Yehudah exhorted them and commanded they should join him.
\p
\v 13 Having consulted privately with¦90453 the¦90454 elders¦90455, he resolved that before¦90458 the¦90460 king's¦90461 army entered into Yehudah \add !(Judea)\add* and¦90467 made themselves masters of the¦90469 city¦90470, they should go out and decide the matter by the help¦90479 of God.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft Some authorities read \fqa the master.\f*
\v 14 And¦90481 committing the¦90482 decision to the¦90484 master\f + \fr 13:14 \ft Some authorities read \fqa Creator.\f* of¦90486 the¦90486 world¦90487, and exhorting those who¦90489 were with¦90490 him to contend nobly even to death¦90495 for laws, temple¦90499, city¦90500, country, and way of life, he pitched his camp by Modi'in.
\v 15 He gave¦90508 out to¦90508 his men the¦90510 watchword, “VICTORY IS GOD’S”, with¦90516 a chosen force of the¦90523 bravest young men he attacked by the¦90527 king's pavilion by night, and killed of his army as many as two thousand¦90532 men, and¦90533 brought\f + \fr 13:15 \ft The Greek text here is probably corrupt.\f* down the leading elephant with him who was in the tower\f + \fr 13:15 \ft Grk. \fqa house.\f* on him.
\v 16 At last they filled the army\f + \fr 13:16 \ft Grk. \fqa camp.\f* with terror and¦90550 alarm, and¦90553 departed with good success.
\v 17 This had been accomplished when¦90557 the¦90559 day¦90560 was¦90562 just dawning, because¦90563 of¦90563 the master's protection that gave Yehudah\f + \fr 13:17 \ft Grk. \fqa him.\f* help.
\s1 The king makes peace
\p
\v 18 But¦90571 the king¦90572, having had a taste of the exceeding boldness of¦90580 the¦90582 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*, made strategic attacks on their positions,
\v 19 and¦90584 on¦90585 a strong fortress of the¦90589 Yehudim at Beyt-Tsur. He advanced, was turned back, failed, and¦90592 was defeated.
\v 20 Yehudah sent the¦90596 things that¦90600 were¦90601 necessary¦90601 to those who were within.
\v 21 But¦90604 Rodokos, from the¦90605 Jewish ranks, made secrets known to the¦90607 enemy. He was sought out¦90610, arrested, and shut up in prison.
\v 22 The¦90621 king¦90622 negotiated with them in¦90624 Beyt-Tsur the second time, gave¦90627 his hand, took¦90628 theirs, departed, attacked the forces of Yehudah, was¦90636 put to the worse,
\v 23 heard that Filippos \add !(Philip)\add* who'd¦90639 been left as chancellor in¦90641 Antiokeia had become reckless, was confounded¦90648, made to the Yehudim an overture of peace, submitted himself and¦90653 swore¦90654 to¦90655 acknowledge all¦90656 their rights, came to terms with them and¦90660 offered sacrifice¦90661, honoured the¦90664 sanctuary and¦90666 the¦90667 place¦90668,
\v 24 showed kindness and¦90670 graciously received¦90673 Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, left¦90674 Hegemonides governor from¦90676 Ptolemais even to the¦90679 Gerrenians,\f + \fr 13:24 \ft The form of this word is uncertain. Compare \fqa Girzites \ft (or \fqa Gizrites), \ft 1 Samuel 27:8. One manuscript reads \fqa Gerarenes.\f*
\v 25 and came¦90682 to¦90682 Ptolemais. The¦90687 men of Ptolemais were displeased at the¦90689 treaty, for they had very great indignation against the Yehudim. They desired¦90695 to¦90695 annul the articles of the agreement.
\v 26 Lusias¦90703 came¦90699\f + \fr 13:26 \ft Grk. \fqa came forward to the tribune \ft or \fqa judgement seat.\f* forward to speak, made¦90709 the¦90705 best¦90705 defence that was possible, persuaded, pacified, gained their good will, and departed to Antiokeia. This was the issue of the attack and¦90719 departure of the¦90720 king¦90716.
\c 14
\s1 Alkimos persuades Demetrios to send Nikanor
\p
\v 1 Three years later¦90723, news was brought to Yehudah and his company that Demetrios the son of¦90734 Seleukos \add !(Seleucus)\add*, having sailed into the¦90734 harbour¦90740 of Tripolis with¦90742 a mighty¦90744 army and¦90745 a fleet,
\v 2 had taken¦90747 possession of the¦90748 country¦90749, having made away with Antiokos \add !(Antiochus)\add* and¦90752 his guardian Lusias.
\p
\v 3 But¦90758 one Alkimos¦90757, who'd formerly been high priest, and¦90763 had wilfully polluted himself in¦90765 the¦90766 times¦90769 when there was no mingling with the¦90767 Gentiles, considering that¦90771 there was no deliverance for him in any way¦90774, nor any more access to the holy¦90781 altar¦90782,
\v 4 came to¦90786 King¦90788 Demetrios in about the one hundred and fifty-first year¦90795, presenting to him a crown¦90798 of gold¦90799 and¦90800 a palm, and¦90803 beside these some of the¦90805 festal olive boughs of¦90808 the¦90808 temple¦90809. Because that¦90813 day¦90812, he held his peace;
\v 5 but¦90817 having gotten opportunity to¦90818 further his own¦90820 madness, being called by¦90826 Demetrios into a meeting of¦90827 his council¦90825, and asked¦90841 how the Yehudim stood affected and what they intended, he¦90840 answered:
\p
\v 6 “Those of the Yehudim called Hasidim,\f + \fr 14:6 \ft That is, \fqa Chasidim.\f* whose leader is Yehudah Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, keep up war and¦90853 are seditious, not allowing the¦90857 kingdom¦90858 to¦90860 find tranquillity.
\v 7 Therefore, having laid aside my ancestral glory¦90865—I mean the¦90868 high priesthood—I've now¦90871 come¦90872 here,\f + \fr 14:7 \ft Some authorities read \fqa a second time.\f*
\v 8 first¦90873 for the genuine care I have for the things that concern the king¦90879, and secondly¦90882 because I have regard also to my own¦90886 fellow citizens¦90887. Because through the¦90892 unadvised dealing of those of whom¦90895 I¦90897 spoke before, our whole race is in no¦90899 small misfortune.
\v 9 Oh king¦90907, having informed yourself of these things, take thought both for our country¦90910 and¦90911 for our race, which is surrounded by enemies, according¦90917 to¦90917 the gracious kindness with which you receive all¦90921.
\v 10 Because as long as Yehudah remains alive, it's impossible for¦90925 the government to find peace¦90929.
\v 11 When¦90934 he'd spoken such¦90933 words as¦90933 these, at once the\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Or, \fqa the \ft king's \fqa friends likewise\f* rest of the king's friends¦90941,\f + \fr 14:11 \ft See 2 Maccabees 8:9.\f* having¦90943 ill will against Yehudah, inflamed Demetrios yet more.
\v 12 He immediately¦90953 appointed Nikanor, who'd¦90955 been¦90956 master of the elephants, and made him governor of Yehudah. He sent¦90963 him out¦90963,
\v 13 giving¦90964 him written instructions to kill Yehudah himself and to scatter those who were with¦90973 him, and¦90977 to set up Alkimos as high priest of¦90980 the¦90980 great\f + \fr 14:13 \ft Grk. \fqa greatest.\f* temple¦90982.
\v 14 Those in Yehudah who had\f + \fr 14:14 \ft See 2 Maccabees 5:27.\f* driven Yehudah into exile thronged to Nikanor in flocks, supposing that the misfortunes and calamities of the Yehudim would be¦91005 successes to themselves.
\p
\v 15 But¦91007 when the¦91008 Yehudim heard¦91006 of¦91009 Nikanor's advance and¦91012 the assault of the heathen, they sprinkled dirt on their heads and made solemn prayers to him who'd established his own people¦91026 forever, and who always¦91027, making manifest his presence, upholds those who¦91032 are his own heritage.
\v 16 When¦91036\f + \fr 14:16 \ft The Greek text of this verse and the next is corrupt.\f* the¦91037 leader had given orders, he immediately¦91040 set out from¦91039 there¦91039 and¦91042 joined battle with them at a village¦91046 called Lessau.
\v 17 But¦91049 Shim'on \add !(Simon)\add*, the¦91050 brother¦91051 of Yehudah, had encountered Nikanor, yet not till late, having been¦91054 delayed because¦91059 of¦91059 the sudden consternation caused by his adversaries.
\s1 Nikanor makes peace with Yehudah
\p
\v 18 Nevertheless Nikanor¦91070, hearing¦91068 of the¦91073 valour of those who¦91075 were with Yehudah, and¦91078 their courage in¦91079 fighting for¦91081 their country, shrank from bringing¦91084 the¦91082 matter to¦91084 the¦91087 decision¦91088 of¦91089 the sword.
\v 19 Therefore¦91092 he sent¦91093 Posidonios, Theodotos, and Matityah \add !(Mattathias)\add* to¦91099 give¦91099 and¦91100 receive¦91101 pledges of friendship.
\v 20 So when these proposals had been¦91105 long considered, and¦91109 the¦91110 leader¦91111 had made the¦91112 troops\f + \fr 14:20 \ft Or, \fqa people \ft Grk. \fqa multitudes.\f* acquainted with them, and¦91115 it appeared that they were all of like mind, they consented to the¦91120 covenants.
\v 21 They appointed a day¦91124 on¦91125 which¦91126 to meet together by themselves. A chariot came¦91129 forward from¦91136 each¦91137 army. They set up seats of honour.
\v 22 Yehudah stationed armed men ready¦91144 in¦91145 convenient places¦91148, in case the¦91151 enemy suddenly betrayed them. They held a conference as was¦91155 appropriate.
\v 23 Nikanor¦91163 waited in¦91164 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, and did nothing to cause disturbance, but dismissed the flocks of people that'd gathered together.
\v 24 He kept Yehudah always¦91180 in¦91181 his presence. He'd gained a hearty affection for the¦91184 man¦91185.
\v 25 He¦91188 urged him to¦91192 marry¦91192 and have children. He married, settled quietly, and took part in common life¦91195.
\s1 Nikanor turns against Yehudah
\p
\v 26 But¦91197 Alkimos¦91198, perceiving the¦91200 good will that was between them, and¦91204\f + \fr 14:26 \ft Or, \fqa and the covenants that had been made, took occasion and came\f* having taken¦91208 possession of the¦91205 covenants that'd been made, came to¦91210 Demetrios and¦91213 told¦91214 him that Nikanor was disloyal to¦91218 the government, for¦91222 he'd appointed that conspirator against his kingdom¦91225, Yehudah, to be his successor¦91227.
\v 27 The¦91230 king¦91232, falling into¦91234 a rage, and¦91235 being exasperated by the¦91236 false accusations of¦91237 that most wicked man¦91245, wrote¦91241 to¦91241 Nikanor, signifying that he was displeased at the¦91246 covenants, and commanding him to send Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* prisoner¦91254 to Antiokeia in all haste.
\v 28 When¦91260 this message came to¦91267 Nikanor, he was confounded, and was very troubled at the thought of annulling the articles that'd been agreed on, the man¦91274 having done no wrong;
\v 29 but¦91277 because there was no¦91281 opposing the¦91278 king¦91279, he watched his time to execute this purpose by strategy.
\v 30 But¦91289 Makabi, when he perceived that Nikanor was behaving more harshly in his dealings with¦91296 him, and¦91298 that¦91299 he'd¦91303 become ruler in his customary bearing, understanding that this¦91315 harshness came not of good, gathered together not a few of his men, and concealed himself¦91317 from Nikanor.
\p
\v 31 But¦91322 the¦91323 other¦91324,\f + \fr 14:31 \ft Or, \fqa though he was conscious that he had been nobly defeated by\f* when he became aware that¦91325 he'd been bravely defeated by¦91327 the strategy of¦91328 Yehudah,\f + \fr 14:31 \ft Grk. \fqa the man\f* came¦91331 to¦91332 the¦91333 great\f + \fr 14:31 \ft Grk. \fqa greatest.\f* and¦91335 holy¦91336 temple¦91337, while the¦91338 priests¦91339 were offering the¦91340 usual sacrifices¦91342, and commanded them to hand¦91345 over the¦91346 man¦91347.
\v 32 When¦91349 they declared with¦91350 oaths that they had no knowledge¦91354 where¦91355 the man was¦91357 whom¦91358 he sought,
\v 33 he stretched out his right¦91362 hand towards¦91363 the¦91364 sanctuary, and swore¦91367 this¦91366 oath: “If¦91368 you won't¦91369 deliver up to me Yehudah as a prisoner¦91370, I'll¦91371 level this¦91372 temple\f + \fr 14:33 \ft Or, \fqa chapel \ft Grk. \fqa enclosure.\f* of¦91376 God¦91377 even with the ground, break down the¦91383 altar¦91384, and¦91386 I'll erect here¦91388 a temple to Dionusos for all to see.
\p
\v 34 And¦91394 having said¦91395 this¦91397, he departed. But¦91398 the¦91401 priests¦91399, stretching forth their hands¦91402 towards¦91403 heaven¦91405, called on him who¦91407 always¦91408 fights for our nation¦91411, in these words:
\v 35 “You¦91415, Oh master of the universe, who in yourself have need of nothing, were¦91420 well pleased¦91421 that¦91423 a sanctuary of your¦91424 habitation\f + \fr 14:35 \ft Grk. \fqa tabernacling.\f* should be¦91428 set among us.
\v 36 So¦91429 now¦91430, Oh holy¦91431 master of all¦91432 holiness, keep undefiled forever this¦91440 house¦91443 that's been recently¦91441 cleansed.”
\s1 The death of Razis
\p
\v 37 Now information was given to Nikanor against one¦91446 Razis, an elder¦91450 of Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, who¦91452 was a lover of his countrymen and a man¦91454 of very¦91457 good report, and¦91460 one called Father¦91464 of the¦91465 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* for his good will.
\v 38 Because¦91469 in¦91470 the¦91471 former times¦91473 when there was no mingling with the Gentiles, he'd been accused of following the Yehudim’ religion, and¦91479 had risked body¦91480 and¦91481 life with¦91487 all¦91488 earnestness for¦91483 the religion of¦91484 the¦91484 Yehudim.
\v 39 Nikanor¦91492, wishing to¦91494 make¦91494 evident the ill will that¦91495 he bore against the¦91498 Yehudim, sent¦91501 above¦91503 five hundred soldiers¦91502 to¦91506 seize¦91506 him;
\v 40 for¦91509 he¦91512 thought¦91508 by seizing him to inflict an injury on them.
\v 41 But when the troops\f + \fr 14:41 \ft Or, \fqa people \ft Grk. \fqa multitudes.\f* were at the point of taking the¦91519 tower¦91520, and¦91522 were forcing the¦91523 courtyard door¦91525, and¦91527 asked for fire¦91529 to¦91530 burn the¦91532 doors¦91533, he, being¦91536 surrounded on every side, fell onto his sword,
\v 42 choosing rather to¦91542 die¦91542 nobly than¦91543 to fall into the¦91544 hands¦91546 of the wicked wretches, and¦91548 suffer outrage unworthy of his own¦91550 nobleness.
\v 43 But¦91555 since he missed his stroke through¦91559 the¦91560 excitement of¦91561 the¦91561 struggle, and¦91564 the¦91565 crowds¦91566 were now rushing within the¦91568 door, he ran bravely up to¦91573 the¦91574 wall¦91575 and threw himself¦91577 down bravely among the¦91580 crowds¦91581.
\v 44 But¦91583 as they quickly¦91584 gave back, a space was¦91586 made, and he fell on the¦91591 middle¦91590 of his\f + \fr 14:44 \ft Or, \fqa the void place\f* side.
\v 45 Still¦91593 having breath within him, and¦91594 being¦91596 inflamed with anger, he rose up, and¦91597 though his blood¦91605 gushed out in streams and¦91606 his wounds were¦91608 grievous, he ran through¦91614 the crowds¦91613, and¦91615 standing¦91616 on¦91617 a steep rock¦91619,
\v 46 when as his blood was¦91624 now well near spent, he drew forth his bowels through the¦91626 wound, and¦91628 taking¦91629 them in both his hands¦91632 he shook them at the¦91634 crowds¦91635. Calling¦91637 on¦91637 him who's master of life¦91641 and¦91642 spirit¦91644 to restore him these\f + \fr 14:46 \ft Some authorities read \fqa the same.\f* again¦91647, he died like this¦91650.
\c 15
\s1 Nikanor's threats and Yehudah's confidence
\p
\v 1 But¦91654 Nikanor¦91655, hearing that Yehudah \add !(Judas)\add* and his company were¦91661 in¦91662 the¦91663 region of Shomron \add !(Samaria)\add*, resolved to¦91664 attack them with complete safety on the day¦91671 of rest¦91670.
\v 2 When the Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add* who were compelled to follow him said, “Don't¦91685 destroy so savagely and¦91688 barbarously, but give due glory¦91691 to the day¦91702 which he who sees all¦91698 things has honoured and hallowed above other days.”
\p
\v 3 Then¦91704 the¦91703 thrice-accursed wretch asked if¦91707 there were¦91708 a Sovereign in¦91709 heaven¦91710 who'd¦91712 commanded to¦91714 keep the¦91716 Sabbath¦91717 day¦91718.
\p
\v 4 When¦91720 they declared, “There's¦91722 the¦91723 master, living¦91725 himself as Sovereign in¦91727 heaven¦91728, who¦91730 told us observe the¦91733 seventh day.”
\p
\v 5 He replied¦91739, “I¦91738 also am a sovereign on¦91741 the¦91742 earth¦91743, who¦91744 commands you to take up weapons¦91747 and¦91748 execute the king's business.” Nevertheless he didn't prevail to execute his cruel plan¦91760.
\p
\v 6 And¦91761 Nikanor¦91764, in his utter boastfulness and arrogance,\f + \fr 15:6 \ft Grk. \fqa carrying his neck high.\f* had determined to set up a monument of complete victory over Yehudah and all¦91766 those who were with him.
\v 7 But¦91778 Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add* still trusted unceasingly¦91781, with¦91783 all¦91784 hope¦91785 that he should obtain help from¦91788 the¦91789 master.
\v 8 He¦91795 exhorted his company not¦91796 to be fearful at the assault of the heathen, but¦91803 keeping in mind¦91805 the¦91806 help¦91823 which in former times they'd often received from¦91809 heaven¦91810, so¦91812 now¦91813 also to look for the¦91815 victory which would come to them from¦91816 the¦91817 Almighty¦91818,
\v 9 and¦91824 encouraging them out¦91827 of¦91828 the¦91828 law¦91829 and¦91830 the¦91831 prophets¦91832, and reminding them of the¦91837 conflicts that¦91839 they'd won, he¦91843 made them more eager.
\v 10 And¦91845 when he'd aroused their courage, he¦91849 gave them orders¦91850, at the same time pointing out the faithlessness of the heathen and their breach of their oaths.
\v 11 Arming each¦91862 one of them, not so¦91863 much with the¦91867 sure defence of shields and¦91869 spears as with the encouragement¦91878 of good words¦91877, and¦91879 moreover relating to them a dream worthy to be believed, he made them all¦91885 extremely glad.
\p
\v 12 The vision of that dream was¦91887 this¦91889: Honyo \add !(Onias)\add*, he¦91890 who'd¦91894 been¦91895 high priest, a noble and good¦91898 man¦91897, modest in bearing, yet gentle in manner and well-spoken, and trained from¦91914 a child¦91915 in all¦91917 points of virtue, with outstretched hands¦91924 invoking blessings on the¦91927 whole¦91928 body of the¦91929 Yehudim \add !(Jews)\add*.
\v 13 Then he saw a man¦91935 appear, of¦91936 venerable age and¦91937 exceeding glory¦91938, and the¦91946 dignity around¦91947 him was wonderful and most majestic.
\v 14 Honyo replied¦91954, “This¦91952 is¦91958 the¦91955 lover of the¦91956 kindred, he¦91957 who prays¦91961 much¦91960 for the¦91963 people¦91964 and¦91965 the¦91966 holy¦91967 city¦91968: Yirmiyah \add !(Jeremiah)\add* God's¦91972 prophet¦91973.
\v 15 Yirmiyah stretched out his right¦91979 hand and delivered¦91980 to¦91980 Yehudah a gold sword¦91983, and¦91986 in giving¦91985 it addressed him thus:
\v 16 “Take¦91989 this¦91990 holy¦91991 sword¦91992, a gift¦91993 from¦91994 God¦91996, with which¦91998 you'll strike down the¦92000 adversaries.”
\p
\v 17 Being encouraged by Yehudah's \add !(Judas)\add* words¦92006, which were noble and effective, and able to incite to¦92011 virtue¦92012 and¦92014 to stir the souls¦92015 of the young to manly courage, they determined not¦92019 to carry on a campaign, but¦92022 nobly to bear down on the enemy, and¦92024 fighting hand to hand with¦92025 all¦92026 courage bring the matter to a conclusion, because¦92032 the¦92035 city¦92036, the¦92038 sanctuary¦92039, and¦92040 the¦92041 temple¦92042 were in danger.
\v 18 Because their fear for¦92045 wives and¦92049 children¦92050, and¦92052 furthermore for family and¦92054 relatives¦92055, was less important to¦92059 them; but greatest¦92062 and first¦92065 was their fear for the consecrated sanctuary.
\v 19 Also those who¦92075 were shut up in¦92076 the¦92077 city¦92078 were in no¦92080 light distress, being troubled because of the¦92084 encounter in¦92085 the open country.
\s1 Nikanor is defeated and killed
\p
\v 20 When¦92088 all¦92089 were now waiting for¦92091 the¦92092 decision¦92094 of the issue, and¦92095 the enemy had already joined battle, and¦92100 the¦92101 army¦92102 had been set in array, and¦92104 the¦92105 elephants\f + \fr 15:20 \ft Grk. \fqa animals.\f* brought back to¦92107 a convenient post,\f + \fr 15:20 \ft Or, \fqa stationed for convenient action\f* and the cavalry deployed on the flanks,
\v 21 Makabi \add !(Maccabaeus)\add*, perceiving the presence of the troops,\f + \fr 15:21 \ft Grk. \fqa multitudes.\f* and¦92124 the¦92125 various weapons¦92126 with which they were equipped, and¦92131 the¦92132 savageness of the¦92136 elephants,\f + \fr 15:21 \ft Grk. \fqa animals.\f* holding up his hands¦92137 to heaven¦92140 called on the¦92142 master who¦92145 works wonders, knowing¦92147 that¦92148 success comes¦92123 not by weapons¦92152, but that, according to how the master judges, he¦92158 gains the¦92160 victory¦92154 for those who¦92163 are worthy.
\v 22 And¦92166 calling¦92167 on¦92177 God, he said this¦92169: “You¦92171, Oh Sovereign master¦92172, sent¦92173 your angel¦92175 in the time of King¦92180 Hizkiyah \add !(Hezekiah)\add* of Yehudah, and¦92183 he killed of the¦92186 army\f + \fr 15:22 \ft Grk. \fqa camp.\f* of Sanheriv \add !(Sennacherib)\add* as many as one hundred¦92190 and eighty-five¦92191 thousand.
\v 23 So¦92193 now¦92194 also, Oh Sovereign of the¦92196 heavens¦92197, send¦92198 a good angel¦92199 before¦92201 us to bring terror and¦92205 trembling.
\v 24 Through the greatness of your arm¦92208 let them be stricken with dismay who with¦92212 blasphemy¦92213 have come¦92214 here against¦92215 your holy¦92217 people¦92219.” As he finished these words,
\v 25 Nikanor and¦92227 his company advanced with¦92231 trumpets and¦92233 victory songs;
\v 26 but Yehudah and his company joined battle with the¦92239 enemy with¦92241 invocation and¦92243 prayers.
\v 27 Fighting with their hands and¦92254 praying to¦92256 God¦92258 with their hearts¦92255, they killed no less¦92262 than thirty-five thousand¦92266 men, being made extremely glad by the¦92267 manifestation of¦92268 God¦92269.
\p
\v 28 When¦92274 the¦92276 engagement was over and¦92278 they were returning¦92282 again with¦92279 joy¦92280, they recognised Nikanor lying dead in full armour¦92287.
\v 29 Then there was¦92288 shouting¦92290 and¦92291 noise¦92298, and they blessed the¦92294 Sovereign master in the¦92296 language of their ancestors.
\v 30 He who in all¦92303 things was in body¦92304 and¦92305 soul¦92306 the foremost champion of his fellow-citizens, he who kept through life the good will of his youth towards¦92316 his countrymen, ordered¦92300 that¦92319 Nikanor's head¦92322 be cut off with his hand¦92326 and¦92324 arm, and that¦92328 they be brought to¦92330 Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 31 When he'd arrived¦92333 there¦92335 and¦92336 had called his countrymen together and¦92340 set the¦92341 priests¦92342 before¦92343 the¦92344 altar¦92345, he sent for those who¦92348 were in the¦92350 citadel.
\v 32 Showing the¦92354 head¦92358 of¦92355 the¦92355 vile Nikanor and¦92359 the¦92360 hand¦92361 of¦92362 that profane man, which¦92364 with proud brags he'd stretched¦92365 out¦92365 against¦92366 the¦92367 holy¦92368 house¦92371 of¦92369 the¦92369 Almighty¦92370,
\v 33 and¦92373 cutting out the¦92374 tongue¦92375 of¦92376 the¦92376 impious Nikanor, he said¦92380 that he'd give it in pieces to¦92381 the¦92384 birds¦92385, and¦92387 hang up these rewards of his folly¦92390 near the¦92392 sanctuary.
\v 34 They all¦92397, looking up to heaven¦92400, blessed the master who'd manifested himself¦92410, saying, “Blessed is he who's preserved his own place¦92411 undefiled!”
\v 35 He hung Nikanor's head¦92418 and shoulder from¦92419 the¦92420 citadel, a clear sign¦92430 evident to all¦92423 of the¦92426 help of¦92427 the¦92427 master.
\v 36 They all¦92433 ordained with¦92434 a common decree to¦92435 in no way let this day¦92442 pass undistinguished, but¦92444 to mark with honour the¦92446 thirteenth day of¦92448 the¦92448 twelfth month (it's called Adar¦92451 in the Arami \add !(Syrian)\add* language), the day before¦92456 the¦92459 day¦92461 of Mordekai.
\s1 The writer's closing words
\b
\p
\v 37 This¦92462 then¦92463 having been the account of the attempt of Nikanor, and the¦92474 city¦92475 having from those times been held by¦92476 the¦92477 Ivrim \add !(Hebrews)\add*, I also¦92479 will here make an end of my book.
\v 38 If¦92486 I've written well¦92488 and¦92489 to the¦92491 point in my story, this¦92493 is what I myself desired¦92496; but if it's poorly done and mediocre, this¦92502 is¦92504 the best I¦92505 could do.
\v 39 Because¦92527 as¦92506 it's distasteful\f + \fr 15:39 \ft Or, \fqa hurtful\f* to drink wine¦92508 alone¦92509 and likewise¦92511 to¦92510 drink¦92510 water¦92514 alone, while\f + \fr 15:39 \ft Grk. \fqa but even as.\f* the mingling of wine¦92520 with water¦92521 at once gives full pleasantness to the¦92526 flavour; so also¦92530 the fashioning of¦92534 the¦92534 language delights the¦92537 ears of those who¦92539 read the¦92541 story.
\p Here¦92543 is¦92545 the¦92546 end.
